window.PGRE = window.PGRE || {}; window.PGRE.modules = window.PGRE.modules || [];
window.PGRE.modules.push({
  id: "em",
  title: "Electromagnetism",
  order: 2,
  greWeight: 0.18,
  prereqs: ["mechanics"],
  blurb: "Unlocks circuits, waves and optics, and about a fifth of the real exam.",
  lessons: [
    {
      id: "em-coulomb-field",
      title: "Coulomb's law and the field concept",
      minutes: 12,
      discovery: "Splitting force into a source-only field E(r) and a response qE lets you precompute the effect of charges once, for any test charge, instead of redoing pairwise sums forever.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "A charged rod is held near a cloud of loose charges — dust, a small charged ball, whatever you drop nearby. You want to predict the force on *anything* you bring close, without re-deriving it from scratch each time. Coulomb's law gives you $F = k\\dfrac{q_1 q_2}{r^2}$ for one pair, but a real setup has many source charges and you keep swapping in different test objects. Recomputing every pairwise force every time you change the test charge is expensive and, worse, doesn't obviously respect the idea that influence should propagate through space rather than jump instantaneously between two distant points." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Pure action-at-a-distance.** Just sum $F = k\\sum_i \\dfrac{q_i q_{\\text{test}}}{r_i^2}\\hat r_i$ fresh every time, for every test charge you care about.\n\n**B. A lookup table.** Precompute the force a *specific* test charge $q_0$ would feel at every point, store it, and reuse the table.\n\n**C. Factor out the test charge entirely.** Define something that depends only on the source charges and the point in space, then multiply by whatever test charge shows up." },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A fails on effort, not correctness:** bring a second, third, fourth test charge and you redo the full pairwise sum every time — no reuse, and nothing is saved between calculations even though the sources haven't moved.\n\n**B fails on generality:** the table was built for $q_0$. Bring a test charge of $2q_0$, or $-q_0$, and the stored forces are wrong by exactly that ratio — you'd need a separate table for every possible test charge, which is worse than A.\n\nBoth A and B also hide a physical worry: they suggest the force appears at the test charge's location *the instant* you place it, with no sense of something already occupying the space beforehand." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "B's failure is informative: the force scales linearly in $q_{\\text{test}}$ (Coulomb's law is linear in each charge), so $F/q_{\\text{test}}$ is *independent* of which test charge you used. That ratio depends only on the source charges and position — exactly the 'source-only, reusable' object C was looking for. Define $\\mathbf{E}(\\mathbf{r}) = \\lim_{q\\to 0} \\mathbf{F}/q$: a vector attached to every point in space, built entirely from the sources. Bring any charge $q$ to that point and the force is just $\\mathbf{F} = q\\mathbf{E}$ — one multiplication, no re-summing. The $q\\to0$ limit matters only in principle, so the test charge itself doesn't disturb the sources; in practice you just use a small $q$." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Electric field** of a point charge $Q$: $\\mathbf{E}(\\mathbf{r}) = k\\dfrac{Q}{r^2}\\hat r$, with $k = \\dfrac{1}{4\\pi\\epsilon_0} \\approx 9\\times10^9\\ \\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}^2/\\text{C}^2$. For multiple sources, fields **superpose**: $\\mathbf{E}_{\\text{total}} = \\sum_i \\mathbf{E}_i$ (they add as vectors, because force does). Force on a charge $q$ sitting in a field: $\\mathbf{F} = q\\mathbf{E}$. Units: $\\text{N/C} = \\text{V/m}$." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "Two charges $+q$ at $(-d,0)$ and $-q$ at $(+d,0)$ (a dipole). At the origin's perpendicular bisector, far away on the $y$-axis, each charge contributes a field of magnitude $kq/(d^2+y^2)$; the $y$-components cancel by symmetry, while the $x$-components add: the field of $+q$ points away from it and the field of $-q$ points toward it, so on the $y$-axis both have equal $+x$ components. For $y \\gg d$, expanding gives $E \\approx \\dfrac{k(2qd)}{y^3}$ — the pair's effect falls off as $1/y^3$, not $1/y^2$, because the near-cancellation of two nearly-coincident opposite charges kills the leading term. This $1/r^3$ falloff of a dipole field, reached purely by superposing two point fields, is a signature you'll reuse constantly." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "The move — split a two-body interaction into a *field* set up by one side and a *response* of the other — is the template for every force field you'll meet: gravity's $\\mathbf{g}(\\mathbf{r}) = -GM\\hat r/r^2$ is the exact same factoring (mass sourcing, mass responding). It's also why superposition works so cleanly here: because $\\mathbf{E}$ is linear in the sources, you can build complicated charge distributions (lines, sheets, spheres) by integrating point-charge fields, which is exactly what the next lesson needs a shortcut for." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "A field $\\mathbf{E}(\\mathbf{r}) = \\mathbf{F}/q$ is defined in the limit $q \\to 0$ mainly because:", options: ["Small charges are easier to manufacture", "A finite test charge would exert its own force back on the sources and perturb them", "Coulomb's law only holds for infinitesimal charges", "The field would otherwise have the wrong units"], answer: 1, explain: "A real test charge pulls on the sources too, slightly rearranging them (especially if they're free to move, like on a conductor). Shrinking $q$ makes that back-reaction negligible so $\\mathbf{E}$ reflects only the original source configuration." },
        { q: "Why does defining $\\mathbf{E} = \\mathbf{F}/q$ (rather than storing $\\mathbf{F}$ for one particular test charge) let you reuse a single calculation for any charge you bring in later?", options: ["Because $\\mathbf{F}$ doesn't actually depend on $q$", "Because Coulomb's law is linear in $q_{\\text{test}}$, so $\\mathbf{F}/q$ cancels that dependence and leaves only the source contribution", "Because $\\mathbf{E}$ is a scalar and easier to store than a vector", "Because charge is always quantized in units of $e$"], answer: 1, explain: "Coulomb's law is $F \\propto q_{\\text{test}}$, so the ratio $F/q_{\\text{test}}$ is exactly the same number no matter which test charge you used — that's the algebraic fact that makes $\\mathbf{E}$ well-defined and reusable." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-gauss",
      title: "Gauss's law and symmetry",
      minutes: 14,
      discovery: "Flux through a closed surface depends only on enclosed charge, so a symmetric enough surface turns a hard integral into algebra — but the shortcut only exists because of that symmetry.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "Find $\\mathbf{E}$ at distance $r$ from an infinite charged sheet, or outside a uniformly charged sphere, or outside a long charged wire. Direct integration — chop the source into point charges, superpose each contribution — works in principle but turns into a genuinely ugly integral for the sheet and wire (you're integrating a $1/r^2$-type kernel over an infinite or extended source with components that don't simply add). You want a shortcut that works whenever the geometry is highly symmetric." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Just grind the superposition integral.** Slice the source into point charges and integrate $\\int k\\,dq/r^2$ component by component, for every geometry.\n\n**B. Guess the field magnitude by dimensional analysis** — e.g. $E \\propto Q/r^2$ always, since that worked for a point charge.\n\n**C. Track something through a *closed surface* around the charge, and see if it's simpler than the field itself.**" },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A works but is punishing** for the sheet (the transverse components from off-axis pieces must be integrated away) and effectively impossible to do quickly, no-calculator, for anything but the simplest shapes — bad news given GRE time pressure.\n\n**B fails outright:** an infinite sheet's field does *not* fall off as $1/r^2$ — a flat infinite sheet looks the same at any distance (no length scale to set decay), so $E$ turns out to be *constant*, independent of $r$. Blind pattern-matching from the point-charge result gives the wrong power law entirely." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "C is the right direction. Picture wrapping any closed surface around the point charge and asking how much field 'pokes through' it — the **flux** $\\Phi = \\oint \\mathbf{E}\\cdot d\\mathbf{A}$. For a sphere of radius $r$ around a point charge $Q$, $\\mathbf{E}$ is radial and constant in magnitude over the sphere, so $\\Phi = E \\cdot 4\\pi r^2 = k Q \\cdot 4\\pi = Q/\\epsilon_0$ — the $r^2$ from the area exactly cancels the $1/r^2$ in $E$. The flux doesn't depend on $r$ at all, and (because field lines from a point charge that don't terminate on it must eventually cross *any* closed surface enclosing it exactly once, net) this holds for *any* closed surface, any shape, as long as it encloses the same charge. That's the invariant we want: $\\Phi_{\\text{closed}} = Q_{\\text{enc}}/\\epsilon_0$, always — but it only becomes a shortcut for finding $E$ itself when you can *choose* a surface (a 'Gaussian surface') on which $\\mathbf{E}$ is constant in magnitude and always parallel or perpendicular to $d\\mathbf{A}$, which requires the source to have matching symmetry (spherical, cylindrical, or planar)." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Gauss's law**: $\\oint \\mathbf{E}\\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = \\dfrac{Q_{\\text{enc}}}{\\epsilon_0}$, for *any* closed surface. It's always true, but only algebraically solvable for $E$ when symmetry lets you pull $E$ out of the integral. Three canonical shortcuts: a sphere for spherically symmetric charge ($E \\cdot 4\\pi r^2 = Q_{\\text{enc}}/\\epsilon_0$), a coaxial cylinder for an infinite line or cylindrical charge ($E\\cdot 2\\pi r L = Q_{\\text{enc}}/\\epsilon_0$), and a pillbox for a sheet ($E \\cdot 2A = \\sigma A/\\epsilon_0$, field on each side)." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "**Infinite sheet, surface charge density $\\sigma$:** take a thin pillbox straddling the sheet, cross-section $A$. By symmetry $\\mathbf{E}$ points straight away from the sheet on both faces (no preferred sideways direction), so flux only escapes through the two end-caps: $\\Phi = E A + E A = 2EA$. Enclosed charge is $\\sigma A$. So $2EA = \\sigma A/\\epsilon_0 \\Rightarrow E = \\sigma/2\\epsilon_0$ — constant, independent of distance, confirming B's failure was the right instinct to distrust. **Sanity check with limiting cases:** between two oppositely charged parallel sheets (a capacitor), the fields add on the inside ($\\sigma/2\\epsilon_0$ each, same direction) and cancel outside — giving $E = \\sigma/\\epsilon_0$ inside, $0$ outside, a result worth memorizing because it recurs constantly." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "This 'exploit the symmetry of a conserved flux' pattern reappears verbatim for gravity (Gauss's law for $\\mathbf{g}$, e.g. why the field inside a uniform sphere grows linearly with $r$) and for Ampère's law later this module, which is the magnetic analogue: instead of flux through a closed surface, it's circulation around a closed loop, and again it's a free shortcut only when the geometry hands you the matching symmetry." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "Why is $\\Phi = \\oint\\mathbf{E}\\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = Q_{\\text{enc}}/\\epsilon_0$ true for *any* closed surface, not just a sphere?", options: ["Because $\\mathbf{E}$ is constant on every possible surface", "Because field lines from the enclosed charge must cross any surface that encloses it a net one time, regardless of the surface's shape", "Because Gauss's law is only an approximation valid for spheres", "Because charge outside the surface contributes zero net field everywhere"], answer: 1, explain: "The total flux only counts how many field lines net escape the enclosed volume, which depends solely on how much charge is inside — the surface's shape is irrelevant to that count, even though $E$ itself is not constant on a non-symmetric surface." },
        { q: "An infinite charged sheet produces a field magnitude that is independent of distance $r$ from the sheet. What is the deepest reason this differs from a point charge's $1/r^2$ falloff?", options: ["The sheet has no charge, only a point charge does", "The sheet has no intrinsic length scale, so there's nothing for $E$ to depend on except the fixed density $\\sigma$", "Gauss's law doesn't apply to sheets", "The permittivity $\\epsilon_0$ changes near a sheet"], answer: 1, explain: "An infinite sheet looks identical under any rescaling of distance (still infinite, same $\\sigma$) — there's no length scale in the problem for $E(r)$ to depend on, so by dimensional necessity $E$ can only depend on $\\sigma$ and $\\epsilon_0$, giving a constant." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-potential",
      title: "Potential: why it beats the field for calculations",
      minutes: 12,
      discovery: "Because the electric field is conservative, you can replace vector superposition with scalar superposition of a potential, then differentiate once at the end — trading a hard vector integral for an easy scalar one.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "You need $\\mathbf{E}$ at a point due to several charges scattered in 3D — say four point charges at the corners of a square, field wanted at the center, or worse, off-center. Superposing $\\mathbf{E}$-vectors means tracking direction and magnitude for every source and adding components — tedious, error-prone under time pressure, and it gets much worse for continuous, irregularly shaped sources." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Grind through vector superposition** every time: resolve each $\\mathbf{E}_i$ into components, add them up.\n\n**B. Add up the scalar *work* needed to assemble the configuration**, hoping it somehow encodes the same information more simply.\n\n**C. Look for a scalar field whose slope reproduces $\\mathbf{E}$**, by analogy with how height determines the direction water flows without needing to specify a 'flow vector' at every point." },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A is just slow** — correct, but it's the source of exactly the arithmetic mistakes (sign errors on components, forgetting to resolve one source) that cost time on a no-calculator exam.\n\n**B sounds promising but is underspecified**: the work to assemble charges from infinity depends on the *path* you imagine bringing them in, unless you can show the force is special — the same worry as for gravity, where you already learned (mechanics) that work done against a conservative force is path-independent. That fact needs to be checked for $\\mathbf{E}$, not assumed." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "$\\mathbf{E}$ from a point charge is radial — it only pushes you directly toward/away from the source, never sideways — so the work to move a test charge from $A$ to $B$ against it only depends on the radial distances at $A$ and $B$, not on the path taken (any sideways wiggle does zero work, since force and displacement are perpendicular there). That's exactly 'conservative,' the same property gravity has. A conservative field is the gradient of some scalar: $\\mathbf{E} = -\\nabla V$. Crucially, $V$ from multiple sources adds as *plain numbers* (superposition of scalars, no components to resolve), and you only differentiate once, at the very end, to recover $\\mathbf{E}$ — so nearly all of a multi-source problem collapses into scalar arithmetic." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Electric potential**: $V(\\mathbf{r}) = k\\dfrac{Q}{r}$ for a point charge (potential energy per unit charge, with $V\\to0$ at infinity), and $\\mathbf{E} = -\\nabla V$ relates them. Potentials from multiple sources add: $V_{\\text{total}} = \\sum_i kQ_i/r_i$. Potential energy of a test charge: $U = qV$. Work-energy: moving charge $q$ from $A$ to $B$ costs $W = q(V_B - V_A)$ against the field (or that much energy is released moving with the field). Units: volts, $\\text{V} = \\text{J/C}$." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "Four charges $+q$ at the corners of a square of side $a$: find $V$ at the center (side length gives corner-to-center distance $r = a/\\sqrt2$). Just add four identical scalar contributions: $V = 4 \\cdot \\dfrac{kq}{a/\\sqrt2} = \\dfrac{4\\sqrt2\\,kq}{a}$ — one line, no components. (Finding $\\mathbf{E}$ at the center directly by superposition would need you to notice by symmetry that the four vector contributions cancel entirely — true here, but the potential route required zero symmetry insight to get $V$, only to interpret that $\\mathbf{E}=-\\nabla V=0$ at a symmetric interior extremum.) This is the general trade: $V$ costs you one differentiation you might not even need (many questions ask only for $V$, or for energy, not $\\mathbf{E}$)." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "Same trick as gravitational potential energy replacing vector gravitational force sums (mechanics module) — anywhere a force is conservative, a scalar potential exists and superposes more cheaply than the vector field. It reappears immediately: capacitor energy, circuit voltage (a circuit's 'voltage' *is* just $V$), and even in mechanics-flavored E&M problems (a charged particle 'rolling downhill' in $V$) where equipotential surfaces play the role of contour lines on a map — motion along them costs no work, exactly like walking along a contour." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "Why does the work done moving a charge between two points in an electrostatic field not depend on the path taken?", options: ["Because $\\mathbf{E}$ is always uniform in space", "Because $\\mathbf{E}$ is radial from each point source, so any component of motion perpendicular to $\\mathbf{E}$ does zero work, leaving only the radial distances at the endpoints to matter", "Because charge is quantized", "Because $V$ is defined to make this true by assumption, with no physical basis"], answer: 1, explain: "A radial field can only do work along the radial direction; wiggling sideways is free. That geometric fact is what makes the field conservative — it's derivable, not assumed." },
        { q: "Which task is scalar superposition (adding potentials) *not* actually faster for, compared to vector superposition of fields?", options: ["Finding the potential energy of a test charge at a point", "Finding the field magnitude and direction directly at a generic (non-symmetric) point, since you still must differentiate $V$ afterward", "Finding voltage between two points on a circuit", "Finding the potential at the center of a symmetric charge arrangement"], answer: 1, explain: "Scalar addition of $V$ is always easier than vector addition of $\\mathbf{E}$ up front, but if you specifically need $\\mathbf{E}$ at a point with no simplifying symmetry, you still pay for a gradient (multiple partial derivatives) at the end — the savings are real but not free for every task." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-conductors-capacitors",
      title: "Conductors, capacitors, and energy in the field",
      minutes: 14,
      discovery: "Charge on a conductor redistributes until the interior field is exactly zero, and pushing charge onto a conductor against its own field stores energy that can be read equally well as living in the charges or as spread through the field itself.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "You place excess charge on a metal sphere, or connect a battery across two metal plates. What field exists *inside* the metal once things settle down, and how much energy does it cost to charge the plates up to some voltage? You need a rule for what conductors do to a field, and a way to count the energy required to build up a charge separation, since simply summing $qV$ for point charges you build up gets confusing when the charges are continuously distributed and mutually interacting." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Assume the field inside a conductor is whatever it 'would be' from the fixed charges**, same as if the metal weren't there.\n\n**B. Assume conductors have no special effect on $\\mathbf{E}$ at all**, since metal isn't charge itself.\n\n**C. Let the charges inside the conductor move freely (they're free electrons) until any net force on them is gone.**" },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A fails immediately**: if the interior field were nonzero, the free electrons inside a conductor (that's what makes it a conductor) would feel a force and *keep moving* — so a static, settled-down conductor can't have this. **B fails for the same reason**: if $\\mathbf{E}\\ne0$ inside, current keeps flowing, contradicting 'static.' Both ignore that 'conductor' specifically means charges are free to respond to whatever field is present." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "C is self-consistent: charges rearrange themselves on the *surface* of the conductor precisely so that their own field cancels any applied field in the interior, wherever they end up. In electrostatic equilibrium there's no leftover force to keep pushing them, so the interior field must be exactly zero, and (since $\\mathbf{E}=-\\nabla V$) the whole conductor is at one constant potential — an equipotential object. All excess charge sits on the outer surface (Gauss's law with a surface just inside the conductor: zero field means zero enclosed charge in that shell). This is *why* conductors are useful for storing charge in a controlled way: two conductors held at a fixed potential difference, separated by vacuum or insulator, is a capacitor, and the ratio of charge stored to voltage applied is fixed by geometry alone." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Capacitance**: $C = Q/V$, units farads. Parallel-plate: $C = \\epsilon_0 A/d$ (from $E=\\sigma/\\epsilon_0$ and $V=Ed$). **Energy stored**: building up charge means each successive bit of $dq$ is pushed against the potential already built by prior charge, $dU = V\\,dq = (q/C)\\,dq$; integrating from $0$ to $Q$ gives $U = \\dfrac{Q^2}{2C} = \\dfrac12 CV^2 = \\dfrac12 QV$ (the factor of $\\tfrac12$ is the signature of this kind of self-built-up energy — same $\\tfrac12$ as spring PE, same origin: linear restoring relationship, integrated). **Energy density in the field**: $u = \\dfrac12\\epsilon_0 E^2$ (can be shown by rewriting $U$ for a parallel-plate capacitor in terms of $E$ and the volume $Ad$ between the plates)." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "Parallel-plate capacitor, area $A$, separation $d$, charged to $Q$: $C=\\epsilon_0 A/d$, $E = \\sigma/\\epsilon_0 = Q/(\\epsilon_0 A)$, and $U = \\tfrac12 CV^2$. Check the field-energy bookkeeping: $u\\cdot(\\text{volume}) = \\tfrac12\\epsilon_0 E^2 \\cdot Ad = \\tfrac12\\epsilon_0 \\left(\\dfrac{Q}{\\epsilon_0 A}\\right)^2 Ad = \\dfrac{Q^2 d}{2\\epsilon_0 A} = \\dfrac{Q^2}{2C}$ — matches $U=Q^2/2C$ exactly, confirming energy can be counted either as 'stored in the charges' or 'stored in the field between the plates,' with the same total. **Limiting case**: pulling the plates apart at fixed $Q$ (disconnected from battery) increases $d$, so $U=Q^2d/(2\\epsilon_0A)$ increases — makes sense, you do work against the attractive force between the oppositely charged plates." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "'Energy lives in the field, not just in the sources' is the conceptual bridge to EM waves later in this module: a wave can carry energy through empty space with no charges anywhere nearby, which only makes sense if $u=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_0E^2$ (and its magnetic twin $u=\\tfrac{1}{2\\mu_0}B^2$) is real and not just an accounting trick. The equipotential-conductor result also reappears in circuits: wires (ideal conductors) are always at one potential end-to-end, which is why you can talk about 'the voltage at a node' without ambiguity." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "In electrostatic equilibrium, the field just inside the surface of a charged conductor is:", options: ["Equal to the field just outside, by continuity", "Exactly zero, because free charges would otherwise keep moving in response to any nonzero field", "Maximum at the center and zero at the surface", "Proportional to the conductor's resistance"], answer: 1, explain: "Free charges inside a conductor rearrange until they cancel any interior field; if any field remained, current would keep flowing and the system wouldn't be static." },
        { q: "The factor of $\\tfrac12$ in $U = \\tfrac12 CV^2$ arises because:", options: ["Capacitors are inherently 50% efficient", "Each increment of charge is pushed against a potential that grows linearly with the charge already present, so the total work is the integral of a linear ramp", "Energy is defined as half the charge times voltage by convention", "Half the energy is lost to resistance while charging"], answer: 1, explain: "$dU = V\\,dq = (q/C)\\,dq$ integrates to $Q^2/2C$ — the $\\tfrac12$ comes from integrating a quantity proportional to $q$, exactly as in spring PE $\\tfrac12kx^2$ where the restoring force grows linearly with displacement." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-dielectrics",
      title: "Dielectrics: why an insulator between the plates helps",
      minutes: 8,
      discovery: "An insulator doesn't conduct, but its molecules still polarize in response to the field, producing a bound surface charge that partially cancels the free field — which is why slipping a dielectric into a capacitor increases capacitance rather than doing nothing.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "You charge a parallel-plate capacitor to some $Q$, then slide an insulating slab (glass, plastic) between the plates, without touching the battery. An insulator has no free charges to redistribute — so naively you might expect nothing to change. Experimentally, though, the voltage across the plates *drops* when you do this, meaning $C=Q/V$ went *up*. Something about the insulator responded to the field even without conducting." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Nothing happens** — insulators have no mobile charge, so the field and everything about the capacitor should be unaffected.\n\n**B. The insulator conducts a little**, leaking charge between the plates and lowering $Q$, which is why $V$ drops.\n\n**C. Each molecule, while not free to move through the material, distorts internally** — its positive and negative charge centers shift slightly apart in response to $\\mathbf{E}$, without any charge actually migrating macroscopically." },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A directly contradicts the observation**: $V$ measurably drops, so *something* about the field between the plates changed.\n\n**B is testable and wrong**: if charge leaked away, $Q$ on the plates would fall (a connected ammeter would show current, and the plates would slowly discharge over time) — but if you disconnect the battery first, $Q$ is fixed by charge conservation on an isolated conductor, and $V$ still drops immediately upon inserting the slab, too fast and too clean to be leakage. The effect is a field response, not a conduction one." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "C survives: each molecule becomes a tiny dipole (or, in polar materials, existing dipoles partially align) — this is **polarization**. Stack up all these aligned microscopic dipoles and the *net* effect, away from individual molecules, is an induced surface charge on the faces of the slab: negative facing the positive plate, positive facing the negative plate. That bound surface charge creates its own field pointing *opposite* to the original applied field, partially canceling it inside the dielectric. Less net $E$ for the same free charge $Q$ on the plates means less $V=Ed$, and since $Q$ didn't change, $C=Q/V$ went up." },
        { kind: "formal", md: `**Dielectric constant** $\\kappa$ (or relative permittivity $\\epsilon_r$), always $\\ge1$: the field inside is reduced to $E = E_0/\\kappa$, and capacitance scales up: $C = \\kappa C_0 = \\dfrac{\\kappa\\epsilon_0 A}{d}$. Typical values: vacuum $\\kappa=1$, air $\\approx1.0006$, glass $\\approx5$–$10$, water $\\approx80$ (water's permanent molecular dipole aligns strongly). At fixed $Q$ (battery disconnected), inserting a dielectric drops $V$ by factor $\\kappa$ and drops stored energy $U=Q^2/2C$ by the same factor (the slab is pulled in and does work on whatever held it, at the field's expense). At fixed $V$ (battery still connected), $Q$ instead *rises* by $\\kappa$ to keep $V$ constant, so $U=\\tfrac12CV^2$ rises (the battery supplies the extra energy).

Bookkeeping this bound charge in general (not just for a slab between flat plates) uses two more fields. **Polarization** $\\mathbf{P}$ is the net molecular dipole moment per unit volume — it's the aligned-dipole density from the discovery above, expressed as a vector field. The **displacement field** $\\mathbf{D}$ is then defined to track only free charge, exactly the way $\\mathbf{E}$ tracks the total (free plus bound): $$\\mathbf{D} \\equiv \\epsilon_0\\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{P}, \\qquad \\oint\\mathbf{D}\\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = Q_{\\text{free,enc}}.$$ For a linear dielectric, $\\mathbf{P}=\\epsilon_0\\chi_e\\mathbf{E}$ (electric susceptibility $\\chi_e=\\kappa-1$), so $\\mathbf{D}=\\epsilon_0\\kappa\\mathbf{E}$ — this $\\mathbf{D}$/$\\mathbf{P}$ split is the direct template the magnetic-materials lesson reuses for $\\mathbf{H}$ and $\\mathbf{M}$.` },
        { kind: "apply", md: "A capacitor charged to $Q_0$, $V_0$ is disconnected, then filled with a dielectric $\\kappa=4$. New voltage: $V = V_0/4$. New energy: $U = Q_0^2/(2\\kappa C_0) = U_0/4$ — three-quarters of the original energy went somewhere; it's the work done pulling the dielectric in (the field does positive work sucking a polarizable material into the region of strong field, same reason a piece of paper is drawn toward a charged comb)." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "This is your first encounter with a **bound charge** that responds to a field without being 'free' — the same logic (a medium polarizes, produces a field that opposes the source) reappears with magnetic materials (paramagnetism/diamagnetism, at a recognition level for this exam) and underlies why light slows down and bends in glass: the glass's bound charges oscillate in response to the light's oscillating $\\mathbf{E}$, radiating a field that lags and effectively slows the net wave — the optical index of refraction is, at heart, a dielectric-constant story." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "A parallel-plate capacitor is charged to $Q_0$ then disconnected from the battery. A dielectric with $\\kappa=2$ is inserted, filling the gap. What happens to the voltage and the stored energy?", options: ["Both double", "Both halve", "Voltage halves, energy quadruples", "Voltage is unchanged, energy halves"], answer: 1, explain: "With $Q$ fixed (disconnected), $C\\to 2C_0$, so $V=Q_0/C \\to V_0/2$, and $U=Q_0^2/2C \\to U_0/2$." },
        { q: "The physical origin of the dielectric constant $\\kappa > 1$ is:", options: ["Free electrons in the insulator conducting a small leakage current", "Bound charge distortion (polarization) of molecules creating an opposing internal field that partially cancels the applied one", "The insulator increasing the effective plate separation", "Quantum tunneling of charge through the insulator"], answer: 1, explain: "Insulators have no free carriers; the reduction in net field comes from molecular-scale polarization producing bound surface charge that opposes the applied field." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-circuits",
      title: "DC circuits: Kirchhoff's rules and RC transients",
      minutes: 15,
      discovery: "A circuit is just charge conservation (current in = current out at every junction) plus potential being single-valued (any closed loop of potential changes must sum to zero) — two accounting rules that pin down every current and voltage, and a capacitor is what makes those currents change smoothly in time instead of jumping instantly.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "A circuit has a battery, several resistors in some tangled combination (not simply series or parallel), and you need every current and voltage. Series/parallel reduction formulas run out once the network isn't reducible that way (e.g. a bridge network). You need a general method that always works, plus you need to know what happens when a capacitor is in the mix and the circuit is first switched on — since $Q=CV$ can't jump instantly if current is finite." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Guess currents by symmetry or intuition** and check if voltages work out.\n\n**B. Try to always reduce to series/parallel**, accepting defeat on non-reducible networks.\n\n**C. Write down conservation laws that must hold at every junction and around every loop, treat unknown currents as variables, and solve the resulting system of equations.**" },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A doesn't scale**: intuition might work for two resistors, but for five resistors in a bridge configuration there's no shortcut guess that's reliably correct, and you can't verify it's right without the underlying equations anyway.\n\n**B concedes defeat exactly where the interesting problems live** — non-series-parallel networks are common on the exam precisely because they can't be shortcut, so 'reduce or give up' isn't a real strategy." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "C works because it encodes two facts that are *always* true, regardless of network topology: charge doesn't pile up at a junction (conservation of charge, applied to a steady state), and potential is a well-defined function of position (so going around any closed loop and adding up every rise and drop must return you to where you started, net zero). Assign an unknown current to each branch, write one junction equation per node (currents in = currents out) and one loop equation per independent loop (sum of IR drops and EMF rises = 0 around the loop), and you get exactly enough independent linear equations to solve for every unknown — no topology-specific cleverness required, just careful bookkeeping." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Kirchhoff's current law (KCL)**: $\\sum I_{\\text{in}} = \\sum I_{\\text{out}}$ at each node. **Kirchhoff's voltage law (KVL)**: $\\sum \\Delta V = 0$ around any closed loop (sign convention: crossing a resistor in the direction of assumed current is a drop $-IR$; crossing a battery from $-$ to $+$ is a rise $+\\varepsilon$). **RC transient**: charging, $\\varepsilon = IR + Q/C$ with $I=dQ/dt$ gives $Q(t) = C\\varepsilon(1-e^{-t/RC})$, so $I(t) = (\\varepsilon/R)e^{-t/RC}$ — current starts at its max value $\\varepsilon/R$ (capacitor initially looks like a wire, zero charge means zero voltage drop across it) and decays to zero (capacitor fully charged looks like a break, all the EMF drops across it). Discharging an initially-charged capacitor through $R$: $Q(t) = Q_0 e^{-t/RC}$. Time constant $\\tau = RC$ sets the timescale." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "**Instant-switch-on limiting case**: at $t=0^+$ a capacitor with zero initial charge has $V_C = Q/C = 0$, so it behaves exactly like a wire (short circuit) for that instant — compute the initial current using only the resistors, ignoring the capacitor's presence in that branch. **Long-time limiting case**: at $t\\to\\infty$ the capacitor is fully charged and carries no current (a broken wire) — compute steady-state currents in the rest of the circuit ignoring any branch that dead-ends in the capacitor. Both limits are found by *replacing* the capacitor with the appropriate ideal element (wire or break) rather than solving the full differential equation — a huge time-saver under exam pressure, since most GRE circuit questions ask only for $t=0$ or $t=\\infty$ behavior, not the full curve." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "The junction/loop method is really 'conservation + single-valuedness' applied to a graph, the same structural idea that makes Gauss's/Stokes' theorems work (a conserved flux through any surface bounding the same charge; a conservative field summing to zero around any loop). The $t=0$ and $t\\to\\infty$ limiting-behavior trick — replace the hard component with its extreme-time ideal limit — is a general exam strategy: whenever a differential equation is expensive but you're only asked about the initial instant or the eventual steady state, check whether those limits can be read off directly without solving the ODE, which you'll do again for LR and LC circuits shortly." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "In an RC charging circuit (battery $\\varepsilon$, resistor $R$, capacitor $C$, initially uncharged), what is the current immediately after the switch closes ($t=0^+$)?", options: ["Zero, since the capacitor blocks all current initially", "$\\varepsilon/R$, since an uncharged capacitor has zero voltage drop and behaves like a wire", "$\\varepsilon/(2R)$", "It's undefined without solving the full differential equation"], answer: 1, explain: "At $t=0$, $Q=0$ so $V_C=Q/C=0$; KVL then gives $\\varepsilon = IR + 0$, so $I=\\varepsilon/R$ — the maximum current in the whole transient." },
        { q: "Kirchhoff's voltage law (sum of potential changes around a loop is zero) is fundamentally a statement that:", options: ["Charge is conserved at circuit junctions", "The electric potential is a single-valued function of position, so returning to your starting point can't net a nonzero change", "Resistors always dissipate energy", "Current must be the same in every branch of a loop"], answer: 1, explain: "KVL is really 'potential is path-independent' (the field is conservative) applied to a closed loop — any net nonzero sum would mean $V$ depends on which way you went around, which is impossible for a well-defined potential. Charge conservation at nodes is KCL, a separate law." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-magnetic-force",
      title: "Magnetic force and its sources: Biot–Savart and Ampère",
      minutes: 14,
      discovery: "A magnetic field can't do work (it always pushes perpendicular to velocity), which is the clue that it must be sourced by *moving* charge rather than static charge, and Ampère's law is the circulation-based shortcut for finding it, playing the same role Gauss's law played for $\\mathbf{E}$.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "A charged particle moving near a current-carrying wire feels a force that curves its path without changing its speed — unlike gravity or the electric force, which can speed things up or slow them down along the direction of motion. You need to know what kind of field produces a force with this odd property, what creates that field, and how to compute it for a wire or coil without redoing a hard integral every time." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Assume it's just another $1/r^2$ central force**, like gravity and the electric force, sourced by some kind of 'magnetic charge.'\n\n**B. Assume static charge alone, sitting still, can source it** — after all, static charge sources $\\mathbf{E}$, so why not this too?\n\n**C. Notice the force is always perpendicular to velocity and hunt for what's special about *moving* charge as the source.**" },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A fails empirically**: no isolated 'magnetic charge' (monopole) has ever been observed — magnets always come in N/S pairs that can't be separated, unlike electric charge which comes in isolable $+$ and $-$. A force with a monopole-style source should let you isolate one pole; you can't.\n\n**B fails a work argument**: if static charge sourced this field the way it sources $\\mathbf{E}$, the force on a nearby moving test charge could point along its velocity (component-wise) same as an electric force does, letting the field do work and change the particle's speed — but experimentally the magnetic force *never* changes a particle's speed, only its direction. That constraint (force $\\perp$ velocity always) rules out anything electric-field-like." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "The 'always perpendicular to velocity' clue points to a cross product: $\\mathbf{F} = q\\mathbf{v}\\times\\mathbf{B}$ automatically satisfies $\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=0$ for any $\\mathbf{v}$, guaranteeing zero work, exactly matching the observation. Turning this around (what sources a field that only couples to *moving* charge?): moving charge itself, i.e. current. A static charge distribution can't source $\\mathbf{B}$; you need current — and indeed, static magnets turn out to be sourced by countless aligned atomic current loops (electron orbital and spin motion), not by any new kind of charge." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Force**: $\\mathbf{F} = q\\mathbf{v}\\times\\mathbf{B}$ (a moving charge in a field) or on a current-carrying wire, $\\mathbf{F} = I\\mathbf{L}\\times\\mathbf{B}$. **Biot–Savart law** (the $\\mathbf{E}$-from-point-charge analogue): $d\\mathbf{B} = \\dfrac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\dfrac{I\\,d\\mathbf{l}\\times\\hat r}{r^2}$ — direct integration, always works but often painful. **Ampère's law** (the Gauss's-law analogue, using *circulation* instead of flux): $\\oint \\mathbf{B}\\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = \\mu_0 I_{\\text{enc}}$, a free shortcut when symmetry lets you pull $B$ out of the loop integral. Canonical result, infinite straight wire: Ampèrian loop = circle of radius $r$ around the wire, $B\\cdot2\\pi r = \\mu_0 I \\Rightarrow B = \\dfrac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi r}$, direction by the right-hand rule (thumb along current, fingers curl in $\\mathbf{B}$'s direction)." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "A long straight wire carries current $I$; find $B$ at distance $r$, then the force per unit length on a second parallel wire carrying $I_2$ at that distance: $F/L = I_2 B = \\dfrac{\\mu_0 I_1 I_2}{2\\pi r}$. **Direction check via right-hand rule**: currents in the *same* direction attract (each wire's field curls around to push the other one inward — worth re-deriving with your fingers rather than memorizing, since sign errors here are a classic GRE trap), opposite currents repel. **Solenoid** (many loops, symmetry along the axis): Ampèrian rectangular loop straddling the windings gives the memorizable $B = \\mu_0 n I$ inside (uniform, $n$ = turns per length), $\\approx0$ outside — directly analogous to the capacitor's uniform $E=\\sigma/\\epsilon_0$." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "The Gauss's-law / Ampère's-law pairing (flux through a closed surface vs. circulation around a closed loop, each with a symmetry-dependent shortcut) is one instance of a deeper pattern in vector calculus (divergence-type vs. curl-type laws) that resurfaces explicitly when Maxwell's equations are collected together in two lessons. The 'moving source, perpendicular force, no work done' signature also reappears for the Lorentz force in crossed $\\mathbf{E}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ fields (velocity selectors, cyclotron motion) — recognizing that $\\mathbf{B}$ never changes speed, only steers, is the fastest way to eliminate wrong answer choices on force questions." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "A charged particle moves through a region containing only a magnetic field. Which statement must be true?", options: ["Its kinetic energy can increase or decrease depending on field direction", "Its speed is unchanged, though its direction of motion can change", "It moves in a straight line unless the field is zero", "It experiences no force unless it is also in an electric field"], answer: 1, explain: "$\\mathbf{F}=q\\mathbf{v}\\times\\mathbf{B}$ is always perpendicular to $\\mathbf{v}$, so it does zero work; speed is constant even though direction (and hence velocity) changes, producing circular or helical motion." },
        { q: "Why can't a static (non-moving, in the frame where it is at rest) distribution of electric charge source a magnetic field, given that it does source an electric field?", options: ["Because $\\mathbf{B}$ requires magnetic monopoles, which static charge doesn't provide", "Because Biot–Savart sources $\\mathbf{B}$ from $I\\,d\\mathbf{l}$ (moving charge), and a charge at rest contributes nothing to that integral", "Because $\\mu_0$ is defined to be zero for static charge", "Static charges do source magnetic fields; this is a common misconception"], answer: 1, explain: "Empirically and via Biot-Savart, $\\mathbf{B}$ is sourced by current ($I\\,d\\mathbf{l}$), i.e. moving charge — a charge sitting still contributes zero to Biot-Savart's integral since $d\\mathbf{l}=0$ for it." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-fields-in-matter",
      title: "Magnetic Materials: Where the Amplified Field Inside Iron Comes From",
      minutes: 14,
      discovery: "Inserting iron into a solenoid multiplies the field with no extra macroscopic current, because the applied field aligns countless pre-existing atomic current loops, whose combined field adds to (or, for a few materials, slightly subtracts from) the applied one.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: `A solenoid carrying a fixed current produces some field $B_0 = \\mu_0 n I$ (from the previous lesson's Ampère's-law result). Slide an iron core into the solenoid, current unchanged, and the field inside can jump by a factor of hundreds or even thousands. No extra current was added anywhere. Where does the enormous extra field come from?` },
        { kind: "candidates", md: `1. The fundamental constant $\\mu_0$ itself somehow increases inside the iron.
2. The iron core is a passive "guide" that redirects the existing field lines into itself, concentrating them, without adding any new field of its own.
3. The applied field aligns microscopic current loops already present in the iron's atoms (electron orbital and spin motion), and the combined field from all these aligned atomic loops adds to the applied field.` },
        { kind: "failures", md: `Candidate 1 fails on its face: $\\mu_0$ is a fixed constant characterizing vacuum, not a property that changes from one material to the next — if it did, "the permeability of free space" wouldn't be a meaningful universal constant at all.
Candidate 2 fails on the specifics of this exact setup: inside an ideal solenoid, the field is already uniform along the whole interior (the previous lesson's Ampère's-law result), with no concentration gradient anywhere for a passive "guide" to redirect field lines into. A redirection story has nothing to redirect here — yet the measured interior field is genuinely hundreds of times larger than $B_0$. That extra flux has to be a real, physical addition, and (checkable independently) it traces to actual bound surface currents that appear on the iron core's surface, not to any rearrangement of pre-existing field lines.` },
        { kind: "discovery", md: `Every atom is already, at the microscopic level, a tiny current loop — orbiting and spinning electrons constitute genuine circulating current, and each loop has its own small magnetic dipole moment. In an unmagnetized material these countless atomic dipoles point in random directions and their fields cancel on average. Applying an external field partially aligns them (directly, for materials whose atoms already have a net dipole moment; or via induced counter-currents, Lenz's-law style, for materials whose atoms don't) — and once aligned, all those tiny atomic current loops act together like an enormous number of extra microscopic solenoid turns, contributing a real, physical field that adds onto (or, for the induced-alignment case, slightly opposes) the applied field. This is not redirection — it's a genuine new source of field, made of already-existing atomic current, unlocked by alignment.` },
        { kind: "formal", md: `**Magnetization** $\\mathbf{M}$: net magnetic dipole moment per unit volume, from aligned atomic current loops. Just as $\\mathbf{D}$ was introduced in the dielectrics lesson to track only free charge, the **H-field** is defined to track only free current: $$\\mathbf{B} = \\mu_0(\\mathbf{H}+\\mathbf{M}), \\qquad \\oint \\mathbf{H}\\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = I_{\\text{free,enc}}.$$ For a linear material, $\\mathbf{M}=\\chi\\mathbf{H}$ (magnetic susceptibility $\\chi$), giving $$\\mathbf{B} = \\mu_0(1+\\chi)\\mathbf{H} \\equiv \\mu\\mathbf{H}, \\qquad \\mu \\equiv \\mu_0(1+\\chi).$$ Three GRE-level categories, by the sign and size of $\\chi$: **diamagnetic** ($\\chi<0$, tiny magnitude $\\sim10^{-5}$, present in *all* materials — induced dipoles always oppose the applied field by Lenz's law, so these materials are weakly repelled); **paramagnetic** ($\\chi>0$, small, $\\sim10^{-3}$ to $10^{-5}$ — atoms have a net dipole that partially aligns with the field, weakly attracted, and this alignment is fought by thermal jostling so $\\chi$ falls with rising temperature); **ferromagnetic** ($\\chi\\gg1$, often hundreds to thousands — neighboring atomic dipoles lock together into large aligned domains, giving enormous amplification, memory of past fields (hysteresis), and a loss of this behavior above a material-specific Curie temperature where thermal agitation overwhelms the domain-locking). Treating a ferromagnet's $\\mu_r$ as one fixed number, as this lesson does, is a linear approximation: the real relationship between $B$ and $H$ in iron is nonlinear and multivalued (the material's response depends on its magnetic history, not just its current $H$) — good enough for exam-level order-of-magnitude estimates, but not the full story.` },
        { kind: "apply", md: `A solenoid with $n=1000$ turns/m carries $I=2\\,\\text{A}$: vacuum-core field $B_0 = \\mu_0 nI = (4\\pi\\times10^{-7})(1000)(2) \\approx 2.5\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{T}$. Inserting a ferromagnetic core with relative permeability $\\mu_r = 1+\\chi = 500$ gives $B = \\mu_r B_0 \\approx 500(2.5\\times10^{-3}) \\approx 1.3\\,\\text{T}$ — the same free current $I$ and turn density $n$, but the aligned atomic dipoles in the core contribute the overwhelming majority of the final field.` },
        { kind: "transfer", md: `This is structurally the same move as the dielectrics lesson: introduce a field ($\\mathbf{H}$, mirroring $\\mathbf{D}$) that tracks only the free source, then account separately for the material's own response ($\\mathbf{M}$, mirroring polarization $\\mathbf{P}$) which adds back into the true field ($\\mathbf{B}$, mirroring $\\mathbf{E}$). Recognizing this electric/magnetic parallel means half of what you need to know about magnetic materials, you already know from the dielectric case — only the sign conventions and the existence of a large, exam-relevant "always dominant" category (ferromagnetism, with no true electric analogue at GRE level) differ.` }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "A material has a small, negative magnetic susceptibility ($\\chi<0$), a response present to some degree in every material. It is best classified as:", options: ["Paramagnetic", "Ferromagnetic", "Diamagnetic", "Non-magnetic, with no response at all"], answer: 2, explain: "Diamagnetism (small negative $\\chi$, induced dipoles opposing the field) is universal — present in every material, though often masked by stronger para- or ferromagnetic effects when those are also present." },
        { q: "For a linear magnetic material with permeability $\\mu=\\mu_0(1+\\chi)$ placed inside a solenoid, the field (compared to vacuum, same free current) is multiplied by a factor of:", options: ["$\\chi$", "$1+\\chi$", "$\\mu_0$", "$1/(1+\\chi)$"], answer: 1, explain: "$B=\\mu H = \\mu_0(1+\\chi)H$, so relative to the vacuum result $B_0=\\mu_0H$, the material multiplies the field by $(1+\\chi)$, the relative permeability $\\mu_r$." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-faraday",
      title: "Faraday's law and Lenz's law: why the minus sign",
      minutes: 12,
      discovery: "A changing magnetic flux induces an EMF, and the induced current always flows to oppose the change that caused it — not because of a separate law, but because energy conservation forbids a self-amplifying current.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "Push a bar magnet into a loop of wire. A current appears in the loop with no battery anywhere — moving the magnet alone causes it. You need a rule for how much current (or EMF) appears, and critically, which *direction* it flows, since the direction turns out to matter enormously (get it backwards and you predict a perpetual motion machine)." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Guess the induced current flows to reinforce the change** — e.g. as the magnet's flux through the loop increases, the induced current adds its own flux in the same direction, amplifying the effect.\n\n**B. Guess the direction is essentially random / depends on the wire's material.**\n\n**C. Demand that the direction be whatever prevents runaway energy gain**, and work out what that requires." },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A fails catastrophically if you follow it through**: if induced current reinforced the change, pushing the magnet in would make the loop's field add to the magnet's, pulling the magnet in *harder*, which would accelerate it, increasing $d\\Phi/dt$ further, increasing the current further — a positive feedback loop generating unlimited energy from nothing. This must be wrong on energy-conservation grounds alone, before even checking experimentally.\n\n**B fails immediately on experiment**: the direction is perfectly reproducible and depends only on geometry and the sign of $d\\Phi/dt$, not on the wire's material (copper vs. aluminum loops show the same direction, different only in magnitude via resistance)." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "C is forced: the induced current must create a field that *opposes* the change in flux — pushing the magnet in, the induced current fights the increase (repels the incoming magnet, meaning you have to do work to push it in, and that work is exactly what becomes electrical energy in the loop). This is self-consistent with energy conservation: work you do against the opposing force is converted, not created from nothing. The 'opposition' isn't a separate physical law bolted onto Faraday's law — it's the only sign consistent with not violating energy conservation, which is why it shows up as a minus sign in the formal statement rather than as an independent rule." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Faraday's law**: $\\varepsilon = -\\dfrac{d\\Phi_B}{dt}$, where $\\Phi_B = \\int\\mathbf{B}\\cdot d\\mathbf{A}$ through the loop. **Lenz's law** is exactly that minus sign, made operational: the induced current's own field opposes $\\Delta\\Phi_B$. For $N$ loops (a coil), $\\varepsilon = -N\\,d\\Phi_B/dt$. Three ways flux can change, all inducing EMF: $B$ changing in time, area changing (e.g. loop expanding or a rod sliding on rails), or the angle between $\\mathbf{B}$ and the loop's normal changing (rotating loop — the basis of AC generators, $\\Phi = BA\\cos\\omega t \\Rightarrow \\varepsilon = BA\\omega\\sin\\omega t$)." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "A conducting rod of length $L$ slides with speed $v$ along rails in a uniform field $B$ perpendicular to the plane: $\\Phi = B\\cdot(Lx)$ where $x$ is the rod's position, so $\\varepsilon = -B L\\,dx/dt = -BLv$ (magnitude $BLv$). By Lenz's law the induced current opposes the rod's motion, meaning $\\mathbf{F}=I\\mathbf{L}\\times\\mathbf{B}$ on the current-carrying rod points *backward*, against $v$ — this is a real retarding (braking) force, and if the rod is released with no external push, it decelerates, converting kinetic energy into resistive heating in the circuit, a clean check that the direction found by Lenz's law is the one that dissipates energy rather than creating it." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "This 'the sign is forced by energy conservation, not an independent postulate' pattern is worth remembering as a check on any induced-effect problem: if your predicted direction would let a system gain energy for free, you have the sign backwards. Faraday's law is also the other half of the Ampère's-law story — moving/changing $\\mathbf{B}$ makes $\\mathbf{E}$, just as (next lesson, and completing the symmetry Maxwell needed) changing $\\mathbf{E}$ will turn out to make $\\mathbf{B}$. This is also the direct mechanism behind transformers, generators, and (via inductance) the 'electrical inertia' you'll formalize next." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "A bar magnet is pushed toward a wire loop, north pole first. By Lenz's law, the induced current in the loop:", options: ["Flows so as to create a magnetic field that repels the approaching magnet", "Flows so as to attract the approaching magnet, accelerating it", "Is zero, since no battery is present", "Flows randomly depending on the loop's resistance"], answer: 0, explain: "The induced current opposes the *increase* in flux from the approaching magnet, which means creating a field that pushes back against (repels) the magnet — consistent with needing to do work to push the magnet in, which becomes electrical energy." },
        { q: "Suppose (hypothetically) the induced current instead reinforced any change in flux. What is the clearest physical reason this can't be correct?", options: ["It would violate charge conservation", "It would create a positive feedback loop generating energy from nothing, violating energy conservation", "It would imply magnetic monopoles exist", "It would make Ohm's law inapplicable"], answer: 1, explain: "Reinforcing the change would strengthen whatever motion or field change caused it, which would strengthen the effect further, snowballing without bound — a free-energy scenario that energy conservation forbids." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-inductance-ac",
      title: "Inductance, LR/LC/RLC circuits, and AC impedance",
      minutes: 16,
      discovery: "A coil resists changes in its own current (self-inductance) exactly the way mass resists changes in velocity, which turns LR, LC, and RLC circuits into direct electrical copies of damped and driven mechanical oscillators you already solved in the mechanics module — so complex impedance is just SHM's phasor trick relabeled.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "A coil is added to a circuit. Its own current creates its own flux through itself, and by Faraday's law, any *change* in that current induces an EMF back on the coil itself. You need to know the sign and size of this self-induced EMF, and then how a circuit with a coil (inductor), resistor, and capacitor together behaves — especially once you drive it with an oscillating (AC) source, where computing $I(t)$ by brute-force differential equations every time is too slow for exam conditions." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Ignore the coil's effect on itself** — treat it as a plain wire, since a wire has negligible resistance and no other circuit element behaves this way.\n\n**B. Assume the self-induced EMF reinforces whatever change in current is happening**, speeding it up.\n\n**C. Apply Lenz's law to itself**: the coil's induced EMF opposes *changes* in its own current, whichever direction that change is." },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A fails to explain a real, measurable effect** — real inductors visibly delay how fast current can ramp up or down (LEDs driven through a coil don't flicker instantly; a coil in an AC circuit visibly shifts current out of phase with voltage), which a plain wire could never do.\n\n**B repeats the same energy-conservation error from Faraday's law**: reinforcing a change in current would let the current run away without bound, again forbidden." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "C is Lenz's law applied one level deeper: since the coil's own flux is proportional to its own current, $\\Phi \\propto I$, and Faraday gives $\\varepsilon = -d\\Phi/dt$, the induced EMF is $\\varepsilon = -L\\,dI/dt$ — proportional to the *rate of change* of current, opposing that change. This is structurally identical to $F=-\\text{(inertia)}\\times a$: inductance $L$ plays the role of mass, current plays the role of velocity, and 'opposing a change in current' is exactly 'inertia opposing a change in velocity.' Once you see that mapping, every mechanical oscillator result you already derived (mechanics module) transfers directly: $LC$ circuits oscillate like a mass-spring system, $RLC$ circuits are the damped oscillator, and driven AC circuits are the driven oscillator with resonance." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Self-inductance**: $\\varepsilon_L = -L\\,dI/dt$; energy stored $U_L = \\tfrac12 L I^2$ (electrical analogue of $\\tfrac12 m v^2$). **Mechanical–electrical dictionary**: $L\\leftrightarrow m$, $I\\leftrightarrow v$, $1/C \\leftrightarrow k$ (spring constant; charge $Q\\leftrightarrow x$), $R\\leftrightarrow b$ (damping). **LC circuit**: undriven, undamped, oscillates at $\\omega_0 = 1/\\sqrt{LC}$ (compare $\\omega_0=\\sqrt{k/m}$). **RLC circuit**: damped oscillation, same qualitative regimes as mechanics (under/over/critically damped) set by $R$ vs. $2\\sqrt{L/C}$. **Driven AC, phasor method**: write $V=V_0e^{i\\omega t}$ and treat each element's opposition to current as a complex **impedance**: resistor $Z_R=R$, inductor $Z_L=i\\omega L$, capacitor $Z_C = 1/(i\\omega C) = -i/(\\omega C)$. Series combination just adds like resistors: $Z = R + i\\omega L - i/(\\omega C)$, and $I_0 = V_0/|Z|$, with current lagging or leading voltage by $\\phi=\\arg(Z)$. **Resonance**: $|Z|$ is minimized (purely real, $=R$) when $\\omega L = 1/(\\omega C)$, i.e. $\\omega_0=1/\\sqrt{LC}$ — same resonant frequency as the free LC oscillation, exactly as a driven mechanical oscillator resonates at its natural frequency." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "Series RLC driven at $\\omega=\\omega_0=1/\\sqrt{LC}$: the inductive and capacitive reactances exactly cancel ($\\omega_0 L = 1/(\\omega_0 C)$), leaving $Z=R$ purely real — current is in phase with voltage and at its maximum possible amplitude $V_0/R$ (as low an impedance as this circuit can have). **Limiting cases worth memorizing**: at $\\omega\\to0$ (DC-like), $Z_C\\to\\infty$ (capacitor blocks, matches the RC intuition that a fully charged capacitor is a break) while $Z_L\\to0$ (inductor looks like a plain wire — makes sense, no changing current to resist). At $\\omega\\to\\infty$, the opposite: $Z_C\\to0$ (capacitor looks like a wire — no time to charge before the field reverses) and $Z_L\\to\\infty$ (inductor fights the rapid change hard, looks like a break). These four limits let you sanity-check or even answer high/low-frequency behavior questions without computing $|Z|$ at all." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "The entire complex-impedance machinery is literally the complex-exponential SHM trick from the foundations module, relabeled: writing $e^{i\\omega t}$ turns differential equations into algebra, and 'impedance' is nothing but 'force per unit velocity response' translated to 'voltage per unit current response.' Once you recognize an LRC circuit as 'spring-mass-damper wearing a costume,' you can import every qualitative result (resonance width $\\propto R$, quality factor, phase lag before/at/after resonance) without re-deriving anything — a major time-saver, and the reason this lesson leans so heavily on the mechanics-module mapping rather than treating AC circuits as a new topic." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "In the mechanical-electrical analogy for oscillating circuits, inductance $L$ plays the role of:", options: ["Spring constant $k$", "Mass $m$ (inertia)", "Damping coefficient $b$", "Displacement $x$"], answer: 1, explain: "$\\varepsilon=-L\\,dI/dt$ mirrors $F=-m\\,dv/dt$-type inertial resistance to changes in current, exactly as mass resists changes in velocity." },
        { q: "A series RLC circuit is driven at a frequency far above its resonant frequency $\\omega_0=1/\\sqrt{LC}$. Which element dominates the impedance?", options: ["The resistor, since $R$ always dominates at high frequency", "The capacitor, since $Z_C=1/(i\\omega C)\\to0$ as $\\omega\\to\\infty$, letting it dominate", "The inductor, since $Z_L=i\\omega L\\to\\infty$ as $\\omega\\to\\infty$, dominating the sum", "None — impedance is frequency-independent"], answer: 2, explain: "As $\\omega\\to\\infty$, $Z_L=i\\omega L$ grows without bound while $Z_C\\to0$, so the inductor's impedance dominates the series sum and the circuit becomes highly reactive (mostly blocking current)." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-maxwell",
      title: "Maxwell's equations: the missing piece and why it was needed",
      minutes: 13,
      discovery: "Ampère's law as originally stated is inconsistent with charge conservation for a charging capacitor, and fixing that inconsistency forces in a new term — the displacement current — which is precisely what allows a changing $\\mathbf{E}$ to source $\\mathbf{B}$, completing a symmetric pair with Faraday's law.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "Apply Ampère's law, $\\oint\\mathbf{B}\\cdot d\\mathbf{l}=\\mu_0 I_{\\text{enc}}$, to a wire charging a capacitor. Pick an Amperian loop around the wire, then ask what surface to use for '$I_{\\text{enc}}$' — Ampère's law technically only says 'current through *a* surface bounded by the loop,' not which surface, and normally any choice gives the same answer. Choose a flat disk punctured by the wire: $I_{\\text{enc}}=I$. Now bulge that surface out so it passes *between* the capacitor plates instead of through the wire: no wire pierces it there, so $I_{\\text{enc}}=0$. Same loop, same law, two different answers — Ampère's law as stated is broken." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Just always use the flat-disk surface and ignore the ambiguity** — pick a convention and move on.\n\n**B. Conclude Ampère's law is simply wrong and needs to be replaced entirely.**\n\n**C. Look at what's different about the bulged surface (between the plates) and find a physical quantity there that could stand in for 'current' consistently.**" },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A dodges the problem instead of solving it** — the ambiguity signals a genuine inconsistency in the law, and choosing to ignore certain surfaces isn't a fix, it's a patch that will fail in problems where no wire-piercing surface is available at all (e.g. a purely oscillating field with no current anywhere nearby).\n\n**B overcorrects**: Ampère's law works perfectly in every steady-current case tested so far (the last two lessons), so the fix should *reduce to* the old law when $\\dot E=0$, not discard everything." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "Between the plates, there's no conduction current, but there *is* a changing electric field (the capacitor is charging, so $E=\\sigma/\\epsilon_0=Q/(\\epsilon_0A)$ between the plates is growing with $Q$). Maxwell noticed that $\\epsilon_0\\,d\\Phi_E/dt$ through that bulged surface exactly equals the conduction current $I$ through the flat surface (both equal $dQ/dt$ by direct calculation), so adding a term $\\epsilon_0\\,d\\Phi_E/dt$ — the **displacement current** — to Ampère's law as a *second* source of $\\mathbf{B}$, alongside real current, makes both surface choices agree. Physically nothing is 'moving' between the plates; it's bookkeeping that restores consistency, but it makes a genuinely new prediction: a changing $\\mathbf{E}$ *alone*, with no current anywhere, sources a $\\mathbf{B}$ field — the mirror image of Faraday's law, where changing $\\mathbf{B}$ sources $\\mathbf{E}$." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Ampère–Maxwell law**: $\\oint\\mathbf{B}\\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = \\mu_0 I_{\\text{enc}} + \\mu_0\\epsilon_0\\dfrac{d\\Phi_E}{dt}$. Collected, **Maxwell's equations** (integral form): Gauss's law for $\\mathbf{E}$ ($\\oint\\mathbf{E}\\cdot d\\mathbf{A}=Q_{\\text{enc}}/\\epsilon_0$), Gauss's law for $\\mathbf{B}$ ($\\oint\\mathbf{B}\\cdot d\\mathbf{A}=0$ — no magnetic monopoles, ever, matching lesson 7), Faraday's law ($\\oint\\mathbf{E}\\cdot d\\mathbf{l}=-d\\Phi_B/dt$), and Ampère–Maxwell (above). Notice the near-symmetry between the last two: changing $B$ makes $E$ circulate; changing $E$ makes $B$ circulate — each is the other's source, with a sign difference and the $\\mu_0\\epsilon_0$ factor being the only real asymmetry." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "For the charging-capacitor Amperian loop drawn between the plates: $\\mu_0\\epsilon_0\\,d\\Phi_E/dt = \\mu_0\\epsilon_0 \\cdot A\\,dE/dt = \\mu_0\\epsilon_0 A \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\epsilon_0 A}\\dfrac{dQ}{dt} = \\mu_0\\dfrac{dQ}{dt} = \\mu_0 I$ — exactly matching what the flat-disk surface gives directly from the wire's conduction current, confirming the fix restores a single, surface-independent answer as required." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "The displacement current is the linchpin that makes the next lesson possible: with both 'changing $B$ makes $E$' and 'changing $E$ makes $B$' in hand, a self-sustaining disturbance can propagate through empty space with no charges or currents at all — each field regenerating the other. That's not a coincidence Maxwell noticed after the fact; it's the entire reason the fix mattered enough to bother making, and it's the direct route to the wave equation for light in the next lesson." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "The 'displacement current' term was introduced into Ampère's law primarily to:", options: ["Account for resistive heating in wires", "Resolve an inconsistency where different (but equally valid) surfaces bounded by the same loop gave different values of enclosed current", "Correct a sign error in the original Ampère's law", "Explain why magnetic monopoles don't exist"], answer: 1, explain: "The flat-disk vs. bulged-surface disagreement for a charging capacitor showed Ampère's law was surface-dependent, which it shouldn't be; the displacement current term restores consistency between any two surfaces sharing the same boundary loop." },
        { q: "Physically, what is 'flowing' as displacement current between the plates of a charging capacitor?", options: ["Real charge migrating through the vacuum gap", "Nothing physically moves there; it is $\\epsilon_0\\,d\\Phi_E/dt$, standing in for a source term that makes the law self-consistent while numerically matching the real current in the wire", "Photons emitted by the plates", "A leakage current through imperfect vacuum"], answer: 1, explain: "No charge crosses the gap between ideal capacitor plates; displacement current is a changing-flux term, not literal charge transport, though it numerically equals $dQ/dt$ in the wire and sources $\\mathbf{B}$ the same way real current would." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-waves",
      title: "Electromagnetic waves: speed, energy, and polarization",
      minutes: 14,
      discovery: "Combining Faraday and Ampère-Maxwell in empty space (no charges, no currents) forces $E$ and $B$ into a self-regenerating wave that needs nothing to propagate through, traveling at a speed fixed purely by $\\epsilon_0$ and $\\mu_0$ — which turns out to be exactly the measured speed of light.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "Is light an electromagnetic phenomenon, or something else entirely (a mechanical wave in some medium, as 19th-century physicists initially assumed)? You have, from the last lesson, that a changing $E$ sources a circulating $B$ and a changing $B$ sources a circulating $E$ — can these regenerate each other with no charges or currents present at all, giving a self-sustaining disturbance, and if so, does it match light's known properties (speed, transverse polarization, energy transport)?" },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. Assume light needs a physical medium** ('ether') to vibrate in, like sound needs air, since every other wave you've studied (mechanics module: strings, sound) needed a material medium.\n\n**B. Assume Maxwell's equations in a vacuum ($Q_{\\text{enc}}=0$, $I_{\\text{enc}}=0$ everywhere) simply give zero fields** — no charges, no currents, nothing to source anything.\n\n**C. Combine Faraday's and Ampère-Maxwell's equations directly** (substitute one into the other) and see what differential equation for $E$ (or $B$) alone falls out, with no assumption about a medium either way." },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A can't be tested from Maxwell's equations themselves** — nothing in Faraday's or Ampère-Maxwell's law refers to a medium at all, only to $\\mathbf{E}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ and the constants $\\epsilon_0,\\mu_0$ (which are properties of empty space, measured with capacitors and current-carrying wires, not of any hypothetical ether) — so assuming a medium imports an assumption the equations never asked for. **B is too hasty**: 'no sources' doesn't mean 'no solutions' — a differential equation can have nontrivial solutions even with zero source term (the trivial $E=B=0$ is *a* solution, but not the only one), the same way $F=ma=0$ still allows constant-velocity motion, not just $v=0$." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "C is the productive move: substituting Faraday's law into Ampère-Maxwell's (both in differential/vacuum form) eliminates one field and leaves a single second-order equation in the other — precisely the wave equation from the foundations module, $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 E}{\\partial z^2} = \\mu_0\\epsilon_0\\dfrac{\\partial^2 E}{\\partial t^2}$, whose solutions are disturbances traveling at speed $v = 1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon_0}$ — with no medium anywhere in the derivation. Plugging in the measured values of $\\mu_0$ and $\\epsilon_0$ (both determined from static field experiments, nothing to do with light) gives $v \\approx 3\\times10^8\\ \\text{m/s}$ — matching the independently measured speed of light to high precision. That numerical coincidence, from constants nobody suspected were related to light, is the strongest possible evidence that light *is* an electromagnetic wave, and that no medium is needed." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Wave speed**: $c = 1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon_0}$. In a medium with permittivity $\\kappa\\epsilon_0$, speed drops to $v=c/n$ where $n=\\sqrt{\\kappa}$ (index of refraction — ties back to dielectrics). **Structure of the wave**: $\\mathbf{E}$, $\\mathbf{B}$, and the direction of propagation are mutually perpendicular (transverse wave); $|E|=c|B|$ at every point, in phase. **Energy flow — Poynting vector**: $\\mathbf{S} = \\dfrac{1}{\\mu_0}\\mathbf{E}\\times\\mathbf{B}$, magnitude $S=EB/\\mu_0=E^2/(\\mu_0 c)$, points along the propagation direction; **intensity** $I=\\langle S\\rangle = \\tfrac12\\dfrac{E_0^2}{\\mu_0 c}$ (the $\\tfrac12$ from time-averaging $\\sin^2$ or $\\cos^2$, same as AC power averaging). **Radiation pressure**: $P = I/c$ (absorbed) or $2I/c$ (reflected) — momentum carried by the wave, $p=U/c$." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "Sunlight at Earth's orbit has intensity $\\approx1360\\ \\text{W/m}^2$. Peak field: $I=\\tfrac12 E_0^2/(\\mu_0 c) \\Rightarrow E_0 = \\sqrt{2\\mu_0 c I} \\approx \\sqrt{2(4\\pi\\times10^{-7})(3\\times10^8)(1360)} \\approx 10^3\\ \\text{V/m}$ — order-of-magnitude estimate achievable with no calculator by rounding $\\mu_0\\approx1.3\\times10^{-6}$, $c\\approx3\\times10^8$: product $\\approx4\\times10^2$, times $I\\approx1.4\\times10^3$ gives $\\approx5.4\\times10^5$, times 2 is $\\approx1.1\\times10^6$, square root $\\approx10^3\\ \\text{V/m}$ — exactly this kind of rough-and-ready estimation is what the timed exam rewards over exact arithmetic." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "'Two coupled first-order laws combine into one second-order wave equation' is the same move the foundations module used to get $v=\\sqrt{T/\\mu}$ for a string from Newton's second law applied to a small element — different physics, identical mathematical skeleton, and the waves-and-optics module (next in the curriculum) leans on this wave equation directly for interference, diffraction, and polarization phenomena. The transverse nature of $E$/$B$ (rather than longitudinal, like sound) is also *why* light can be polarized at all — a property you'll formalize with Malus's law there." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "The speed of light emerging from Maxwell's equations as $c=1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon_0}$ is significant mainly because:", options: ["$\\mu_0$ and $\\epsilon_0$ were defined using light in the first place, so this is circular", "$\\mu_0$ and $\\epsilon_0$ were measured from purely static/steady-current experiments unrelated to light, yet their combination matches light's independently measured speed", "It proves light needs a medium to propagate through", "It shows Maxwell's equations only apply to visible light, not other frequencies"], answer: 1, explain: "$\\mu_0$ comes from force-between-wires experiments, $\\epsilon_0$ from force-between-charges experiments — nothing to do with optics — so the numerical match to $c$ was strong evidence, not a built-in coincidence, that light is an EM wave." },
        { q: "In a plane EM wave traveling in vacuum, which relationship between the field magnitudes holds at every instant?", options: ["$E = cB$", "$E = B/c$", "$E$ and $B$ are unrelated in magnitude, only in direction", "$E = \\mu_0\\epsilon_0 B$"], answer: 0, explain: "From the wave solution to Maxwell's equations, $E=cB$ at every point and time — the two fields are locked together in both phase and relative magnitude, not independent." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "em-multipoles",
      title: "Multipoles and the method of images (recognition level)",
      minutes: 8,
      discovery: "Any complicated charge distribution's far-away potential is well-approximated by the leading nonzero term of an expansion in $1/r$ powers (monopole, dipole, quadrupole, ...), and for a charge near a grounded conductor, a fictitious 'image charge' behind the surface reproduces the true boundary condition without ever solving the exact geometry.",
      sections: [
        { kind: "problem", md: "Two situations that look intractable at first glance: (1) you need the far-field potential of some lumpy, irregular charge distribution — computing the exact integral for every point is overkill if you only care about behavior far away; (2) a point charge sits near a large grounded (flat) conducting plane, and you need the field in the region above the plane, where the conductor's induced surface charge is unknown and depends on the point charge's own position in a way that seems circular to solve directly." },
        { kind: "candidates", md: "**A. For (1), always keep the full exact expression** — no approximation, exact but often useless in closed form for irregular sources.\n\n**B. For (2), attempt to directly solve for the induced surface charge density** by demanding the conductor's surface is an equipotential, from scratch, for every geometry.\n\n**C. For both: replace the hard real problem with a simpler *equivalent* one that reproduces the only quantity you actually need (the far field; the boundary condition), even if the equivalent problem isn't physically what's happening.**" },
        { kind: "failures", md: "**A is correct but not useful under time pressure** — an exact irregular-source integral is rarely doable by hand in 1-2 minutes; you need the leading-order far-field behavior, not the exact answer, for almost every GRE-style question about complex sources.\n\n**B is solvable with real math (image method, below), but 'from scratch' work** — treating it as a fresh boundary-value problem every time — throws away the pattern-recognition shortcut that makes these problems fast once you've seen the trick once." },
        { kind: "discovery", md: "For (1): far from *any* localized source, $V(r)$ can be expanded in powers of $1/r$. The $1/r$ term ('monopole') survives only if net charge is nonzero; if net charge is zero (e.g. a dipole, or any neutral molecule), the leading term is $1/r^2$ ('dipole,' matching the field's $1/r^3$ falloff you found in lesson 1); if the dipole moment also vanishes by symmetry, the next term is $1/r^3$ ('quadrupole'), and so on — each successive term falls off one power faster and matters only if all lower terms vanish by symmetry. For (2): claim that the induced charge on the grounded plane produces *exactly* the same field (above the plane) as would a single fictitious charge $-q$ placed at the mirror-image point behind the plane. Check it satisfies the one condition that matters — $V=0$ everywhere on the plane, since it's grounded — and by uniqueness of solutions to this kind of boundary problem, if any configuration satisfies the boundary condition and the source above the plane, it's *the* answer, however you found it." },
        { kind: "formal", md: "**Multipole hierarchy**: monopole ($V\\propto1/r$, needs net charge $Q\\ne0$), dipole ($V\\propto1/r^2$, characterized by dipole moment $p=qd$, needs $Q=0$ but charge separation), quadrupole ($V\\propto1/r^3$, needs $Q=0$ and $p=0$). Which term dominates far away is entirely a symmetry question, not a calculation — check net charge first, then whether the arrangement has a net dipole moment, etc. **Method of images**: point charge $+q$ at height $d$ above an infinite grounded conducting plane is equivalent, for $z>0$, to $+q$ at $d$ and image charge $-q$ at $-d$ with the plane removed; induced surface charge and the attractive force on the real charge, $F = \\dfrac{kq^2}{(2d)^2}$ (attractive, toward the plane), follow immediately from the image dipole's field." },
        { kind: "apply", md: "A water molecule (net neutral, but polar) at large distance: leading term is the dipole term, $V\\propto p\\cos\\theta/r^2$ — recognizing 'neutral overall, but internal charge separation' tells you immediately (no calculation) that you're in $1/r^2$/$1/r^3$ territory, not $1/r$, which is often all a GRE question is testing. For the grounded-plane charge: force of attraction is $kq^2/4d^2$ — note this is the *same functional form* as two real point charges $q$ and $-q$ separated by $2d$, purely because the image method guarantees the field above the plane looks exactly like that two-charge configuration, even though the actual physical charge below the plane is a spread-out induced surface charge, not a point charge at all." },
        { kind: "transfer", md: "Both tricks are instances of the same idea: when the exact source is complicated but a much simpler *fictitious* source reproduces the one boundary condition or far-field behavior you actually need, use the fictitious source — uniqueness theorems (not proven here, just used) guarantee this is legitimate, not a cheat. You'll see the multipole hierarchy's logic again implicitly in atomic and nuclear physics (selection rules, which multipole order a transition proceeds through) and the image-charge trick generalizes to spheres and other conductor shapes, though for the GRE, recognizing *when* net charge/dipole moment vanish (to identify the leading multipole) and recalling the flat-plane image result are the testable skills, not deriving new geometries from scratch." }
      ],
      checkpoints: [
        { q: "A charge distribution has zero net charge but a nonzero dipole moment. Far away, its potential falls off as:", options: ["$1/r$", "$1/r^2$", "$1/r^3$", "It is exactly zero everywhere"], answer: 1, explain: "Zero net charge kills the monopole ($1/r$) term; a nonzero dipole moment means the next term in the expansion, $\\propto1/r^2$, is the leading nonvanishing one." },
        { q: "A point charge $+q$ is held at height $d$ above an infinite grounded conducting plane. The force pulling it toward the plane has the same magnitude as the force between two point charges $q$ and $-q$ separated by:", options: ["$d$", "$2d$", "$d/2$", "$4d$"], answer: 1, explain: "The image charge $-q$ sits at $-d$ (mirror position), a distance $2d$ below the real charge at $+d$, so the equivalent two-charge separation is $2d$, giving $F=kq^2/(2d)^2$." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  drills: [
    { id: "em-coulomb-field-d1", lesson: "em-coulomb-field", type: "mc", prompt: "Two point charges separated by $r$ feel force $F$. If the separation is doubled (charges unchanged), the new force is:", options: ["$F/4$", "$F/2$", "$2F$", "$4F$"], answer: 0, hint: "Coulomb force $\\propto 1/r^2$.", solution: "$F\\propto 1/r^2$, so doubling $r$ divides $F$ by $2^2=4$.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["coulomb", "inverse-square"] },
    { id: "em-coulomb-field-d2", lesson: "em-coulomb-field", type: "mc", prompt: "A point charge produces field $E$ at distance $r$. At distance $3r$, the field is:", options: ["$9E$", "$3E$", "$E/3$", "$E/9$"], answer: 3, hint: "$E\\propto 1/r^2$.", solution: "Tripling distance divides field by $3^2=9$: $E/9$.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["coulomb", "inverse-square"] },
    { id: "em-coulomb-field-d3", lesson: "em-coulomb-field", type: "mc", prompt: "Far from a dipole, along its axis, the field magnitude falls off as:", options: ["$1/r$", "$1/r^2$", "$1/r^3$", "$1/r^4$"], answer: 2, hint: "The two charges' $1/r^2$ contributions nearly cancel.", solution: "Superposing the two nearly-coincident opposite point charges cancels the leading $1/r^2$ term, leaving $1/r^3$ as the leading behavior.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["dipole", "superposition"] },
    { id: "em-coulomb-field-d4", lesson: "em-coulomb-field", type: "numeric", prompt: "Find the magnitude of the electric field $0.50\\,\\text{m}$ from a point charge of $2\\,\\mu\\text{C}$.", answer: 72000, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "N/C", hint: "$E = kQ/r^2$.", solution: "$E = (9\\times10^9)(2\\times10^{-6})/(0.5)^2 = (1.8\\times10^4)/0.25 = 7.2\\times10^4\\ \\text{N/C}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["coulomb", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-gauss-d1", lesson: "em-gauss", type: "mc", prompt: "Gauss's law states that electric flux through any closed surface equals:", options: ["Enclosed charge divided by $\\epsilon_0$", "Total charge in the universe divided by $\\epsilon_0$", "Surface charge density times area", "Enclosed current times $\\mu_0$"], answer: 0, hint: "$\\oint\\mathbf{E}\\cdot d\\mathbf{A} = Q_{\\text{enc}}/\\epsilon_0$.", solution: "Only enclosed charge matters; charge outside contributes zero net flux.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["gauss-law"] },
    { id: "em-gauss-d2", lesson: "em-gauss", type: "mc", prompt: "The field of an infinite charged sheet is independent of distance from the sheet mainly because:", options: ["Gauss's law is only approximate for sheets", "An infinite sheet has no intrinsic length scale for $E(r)$ to depend on", "$\\epsilon_0$ varies with distance", "Charge cannot exist on an infinite sheet"], answer: 1, hint: "What length scales exist in the problem?", solution: "With only $\\sigma$ and $\\epsilon_0$ available (no length scale), dimensional analysis forces $E$ to be constant.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["gauss-law", "symmetry"] },
    { id: "em-gauss-d3", lesson: "em-gauss", type: "numeric", prompt: "Find the field magnitude just outside an infinite sheet with surface charge density $4\\,\\mu\\text{C/m}^2$.", answer: 226000, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "N/C", hint: "$E = \\sigma/2\\epsilon_0$.", solution: "$E = \\dfrac{4\\times10^{-6}}{2(8.85\\times10^{-12})} \\approx 2.26\\times10^5\\ \\text{N/C}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["gauss-law", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-gauss-d4", lesson: "em-gauss", type: "mc", prompt: "Between two infinite parallel sheets carrying equal and opposite surface charge density $\\sigma$, the field magnitude in the gap is:", options: ["$\\sigma/\\epsilon_0$", "$\\sigma/2\\epsilon_0$", "$0$", "$2\\sigma/\\epsilon_0$"], answer: 0, hint: "The two sheets' fields add inside the gap.", solution: "Each sheet contributes $\\sigma/2\\epsilon_0$ in the same direction inside the gap: total $\\sigma/\\epsilon_0$; outside they cancel to zero.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["gauss-law", "capacitor"] },
    { id: "em-potential-d1", lesson: "em-potential", type: "mc", prompt: "The work done moving a charge along an equipotential surface is:", options: ["Zero", "Equal to $qV$", "Path-dependent", "Always negative"], answer: 0, hint: "$W = q\\Delta V$.", solution: "On an equipotential surface $\\Delta V = 0$, so $W = q\\Delta V = 0$.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["potential"] },
    { id: "em-potential-d2", lesson: "em-potential", type: "mc", prompt: "Given $V(r) = kQ/r$ for a point charge, the field magnitude $E=-dV/dr$ is:", options: ["$kQ/r^2$", "$-kQ/r^2$", "$kQ/r$", "$kQ\\ln r$"], answer: 0, hint: "Differentiate $V=kQ/r$ with respect to $r$, then negate.", solution: "$dV/dr = -kQ/r^2$, so $E=-dV/dr = kQ/r^2$ (magnitude, pointing outward for positive $Q$).", difficulty: 2, tags: ["potential", "gradient"] },
    { id: "em-potential-d3", lesson: "em-potential", type: "numeric", prompt: "Find the potential at $0.20\\,\\text{m}$ from a point charge of $5\\,\\text{nC}$.", answer: 225, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "V", hint: "$V=kQ/r$.", solution: "$V = (9\\times10^9)(5\\times10^{-9})/0.2 = 45/0.2 = 225\\ \\text{V}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["potential", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-potential-d4", lesson: "em-potential", type: "mc", prompt: "For a dipole ($+q$ and $-q$ separated by $d$), the potential at any point on the perpendicular bisector of the line joining them is:", options: ["Zero, since both charges are equidistant and their contributions cancel", "Equal to $kq/r$", "Equal to $2kq/r$", "Undefined"], answer: 0, hint: "$V$ is a scalar; equal distances mean equal-magnitude, opposite-sign contributions.", solution: "Both charges are the same distance from any point on the bisector, contributing $+kq/r$ and $-kq/r$, which cancel exactly.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["potential", "dipole"] },
    { id: "em-conductors-capacitors-d1", lesson: "em-conductors-capacitors", type: "mc", prompt: "In electrostatic equilibrium, the electric field just inside the surface of a charged conductor is:", options: ["Zero", "Equal to $\\sigma/\\epsilon_0$", "Equal to $\\sigma/2\\epsilon_0$", "Maximum at the center"], answer: 0, hint: "Free charges would keep moving if the field were nonzero.", solution: "Static equilibrium requires zero net force on interior free charges, so $E_{\\text{inside}} = 0$.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["conductors"] },
    { id: "em-conductors-capacitors-d2", lesson: "em-conductors-capacitors", type: "numeric", prompt: "A parallel-plate capacitor has area $0.010\\,\\text{m}^2$ and plate separation $1.0\\,\\text{mm}$. Find its capacitance.", answer: 8.85e-11, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "F", hint: "$C = \\epsilon_0 A/d$.", solution: "$C = (8.85\\times10^{-12})(0.010)/(0.001) = 8.85\\times10^{-11}\\ \\text{F}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["capacitance", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-conductors-capacitors-d3", lesson: "em-conductors-capacitors", type: "mc", prompt: "If the voltage across a capacitor (fixed $C$) is doubled, the stored energy:", options: ["Doubles", "Quadruples", "Halves", "Stays the same"], answer: 1, hint: "$U = \\tfrac12 CV^2$.", solution: "$U\\propto V^2$, so doubling $V$ quadruples $U$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["capacitance", "energy"] },
    { id: "em-conductors-capacitors-d4", lesson: "em-conductors-capacitors", type: "mc", prompt: "A charged parallel-plate capacitor is disconnected from its battery (charge $Q$ fixed), then the plates are pulled further apart. The stored energy:", options: ["Increases, since $U=Q^2/2C$ and $C$ decreases as $d$ increases", "Decreases, since separating the plates does negative work", "Stays constant, since $Q$ is fixed", "Becomes zero"], answer: 0, hint: "How does $C=\\epsilon_0A/d$ change as $d$ increases, at fixed $Q$?", solution: "$C=\\epsilon_0A/d$ decreases as $d$ grows; with $Q$ fixed, $U=Q^2/2C$ increases — you do positive work pulling the attracting plates apart.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["capacitance", "energy", "limiting-case"] },
    { id: "em-dielectrics-d1", lesson: "em-dielectrics", type: "mc", prompt: "A charged capacitor (fixed $Q$, battery disconnected) has a dielectric slab inserted between its plates. The voltage:", options: ["Decreases", "Increases", "Stays the same", "Becomes zero"], answer: 0, hint: "$C$ increases; $Q$ is fixed.", solution: "$C\\to\\kappa C_0$ increases while $Q$ is fixed, so $V=Q/C$ decreases by factor $\\kappa$.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["dielectrics"] },
    { id: "em-dielectrics-d2", lesson: "em-dielectrics", type: "mc", prompt: "A capacitor stays connected to a battery (fixed $V$) while a dielectric is inserted. The charge on the plates:", options: ["Increases", "Decreases", "Stays the same", "Drops to zero"], answer: 0, hint: "$C$ increases; $V$ is held fixed by the battery.", solution: "$Q = CV$ with $V$ fixed and $C\\to\\kappa C_0$: charge increases by factor $\\kappa$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["dielectrics"] },
    { id: "em-dielectrics-d3", lesson: "em-dielectrics", type: "numeric", prompt: "A capacitor with vacuum capacitance $2\\,\\text{pF}$ is filled with a dielectric of $\\kappa=5$. Find the new capacitance.", answer: 10, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "pF", hint: "$C=\\kappa C_0$.", solution: "$C = 5\\times2\\,\\text{pF} = 10\\,\\text{pF}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["dielectrics", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-dielectrics-d4", lesson: "em-dielectrics", type: "mc", prompt: "The microscopic origin of a dielectric constant $\\kappa>1$ is:", options: ["Bound charge polarization of molecules opposing the applied field", "Free electron conduction through the insulator", "The insulator increasing the effective plate area", "Quantum tunneling between plates"], answer: 0, hint: "Insulators have no free carriers.", solution: "Molecular dipoles (induced or aligned) create bound surface charge that partially cancels the applied field — that's polarization, not conduction.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["dielectrics", "polarization"] },
    { id: "em-circuits-d1", lesson: "em-circuits", type: "mc", prompt: "Kirchhoff's current law at a circuit junction states that:", options: ["Current flowing in equals current flowing out", "Voltage around any loop sums to zero", "Resistance in series always adds", "Power is conserved only in resistors"], answer: 0, hint: "It's charge conservation at a node.", solution: "KCL is charge conservation applied to a junction: no charge accumulates, so current in equals current out.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["kirchhoff"] },
    { id: "em-circuits-d2", lesson: "em-circuits", type: "numeric", prompt: "An RC circuit has $R=2.0\\,\\text{k}\\Omega$ and $C=5.0\\,\\mu\\text{F}$. Find the time constant.", answer: 0.01, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "s", hint: "$\\tau = RC$.", solution: "$\\tau = (2000)(5\\times10^{-6}) = 0.01\\,\\text{s} = 10\\,\\text{ms}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["rc-circuit", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-circuits-d3", lesson: "em-circuits", type: "mc", prompt: "In a series RC circuit charging from an initially uncharged capacitor, the current immediately after the switch closes ($t=0^+$) is:", options: ["$\\varepsilon/R$", "Zero", "$\\varepsilon/2R$", "Undefined without solving the ODE"], answer: 0, hint: "An uncharged capacitor briefly looks like a wire.", solution: "$Q=0 \\Rightarrow V_C=0$, so all of $\\varepsilon$ drops across $R$: $I=\\varepsilon/R$, the maximum current of the whole transient.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["rc-circuit", "limiting-case"] },
    { id: "em-circuits-d4", lesson: "em-circuits", type: "mc", prompt: "In the same charging circuit, the current a very long time after the switch closes ($t\\to\\infty$) is:", options: ["Zero, since the fully charged capacitor blocks further current", "$\\varepsilon/R$, unchanged from $t=0$", "$\\varepsilon/2R$", "Infinite"], answer: 0, hint: "A fully charged capacitor behaves like a break in the circuit.", solution: "As $t\\to\\infty$, $Q\\to C\\varepsilon$ so $V_C\\to\\varepsilon$, leaving zero voltage across $R$ and hence $I\\to0$.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["rc-circuit", "limiting-case"] },
    { id: "em-magnetic-force-d1", lesson: "em-magnetic-force", type: "mc", prompt: "The magnetic force on a moving charge, $\\mathbf{F}=q\\mathbf{v}\\times\\mathbf{B}$, does work on the charge equal to:", options: ["Zero, always", "$qvB$", "$qvB\\sin\\theta$", "It depends on the sign of $q$"], answer: 0, hint: "$\\mathbf{F}$ is always perpendicular to $\\mathbf{v}$.", solution: "$\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = q(\\mathbf{v}\\times\\mathbf{B})\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = 0$ always, since a cross product is perpendicular to both original vectors.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["magnetic-force"] },
    { id: "em-magnetic-force-d2", lesson: "em-magnetic-force", type: "numeric", prompt: "Find the magnetic field magnitude at $5.0\\,\\text{cm}$ from a long straight wire carrying $10\\,\\text{A}$.", answer: 0.00004, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "T", hint: "$B=\\mu_0 I/2\\pi r$.", solution: "$B = \\dfrac{(4\\pi\\times10^{-7})(10)}{2\\pi(0.05)} = \\dfrac{2\\times10^{-7}\\times10}{0.05} = 4\\times10^{-5}\\ \\text{T}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["biot-savart", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-magnetic-force-d3", lesson: "em-magnetic-force", type: "mc", prompt: "Two long parallel wires carry current in the same direction. They:", options: ["Attract each other", "Repel each other", "Feel no force from each other", "Attract or repel depending on wire material"], answer: 0, hint: "Use the right-hand rule for each wire's field at the other's location.", solution: "Each wire's field curls to push the other one inward — same-direction currents attract, opposite-direction currents repel.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["ampere", "right-hand-rule"] },
    { id: "em-magnetic-force-d4", lesson: "em-magnetic-force", type: "mc", prompt: "Inside a long solenoid with $n$ turns per unit length carrying current $I$, the field is approximately:", options: ["$\\mu_0 n I$, uniform", "$\\mu_0 I/2\\pi r$, decreasing with distance from the axis", "Zero", "$\\mu_0 n I^2$"], answer: 0, hint: "Use an Amperian loop straddling the windings.", solution: "A rectangular Amperian loop gives $B\\cdot L = \\mu_0 n L I \\Rightarrow B=\\mu_0 nI$, uniform inside and (ideally) zero outside.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["ampere", "solenoid"] },
    { id: "em-fields-in-matter-d1", lesson: "em-fields-in-matter", type: "mc", prompt: "The $\\mathbf{H}$-field in a magnetic material is defined so that Ampère's law, $\\oint\\mathbf{H}\\cdot d\\mathbf{l}=I_{\\text{enc}}$, involves only:", options: ["Bound current from aligned atomic dipoles", "Total current, free and bound combined", "Displacement current only", "Free (externally supplied) current"], answer: 3, hint: "This mirrors how $\\mathbf{D}$ tracks only free charge in dielectrics.", solution: "$\\mathbf{H}$ is defined precisely so its circulation depends only on free current, with the material's own contribution folded into $\\mathbf{M}$ instead.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["magnetic-materials"] },
    { id: "em-fields-in-matter-d2", lesson: "em-fields-in-matter", type: "numeric", prompt: "A solenoid has $n=800\\,\\text{turns/m}$ carrying $I=3\\,\\text{A}$, with a core of relative permeability $\\mu_r=200$. Find the field magnitude inside the core, in T.", answer: 0.603, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "T", hint: "$B=\\mu_r\\mu_0 nI$.", solution: "$B_0=\\mu_0nI=(4\\pi\\times10^{-7})(800)(3)\\approx3.02\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{T}$; $B=\\mu_rB_0=200(3.02\\times10^{-3})\\approx0.603\\,\\text{T}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["magnetic-materials", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-fields-in-matter-d3", lesson: "em-fields-in-matter", type: "mc", prompt: "A solenoid with $n$ turns per unit length carries free current $I$ and is filled uniformly with a linear magnetic core. Using $\\oint\\mathbf{H}\\cdot d\\mathbf{l}=I_{\\text{free,enc}}$, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{H}$ inside is:", options: ["$\\mu_r nI$", "$nI/\\mu_r$", "Zero, since $\\mathbf{H}$ only exists in vacuum", "$nI$, independent of the core material"], answer: 3, hint: "$\\mathbf{H}$'s circulation depends only on the free current, which hasn't changed.", solution: "Ampère's law for $\\mathbf{H}$ only sees free current, exactly as in the empty solenoid, so $H=nI$ regardless of what core is inserted — all of the core's effect shows up in $\\mathbf{M}$ (and hence $B=\\mu_r\\mu_0 nI$), not in $H$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["magnetic-materials"] },
    { id: "em-fields-in-matter-d4", lesson: "em-fields-in-matter", type: "mc", prompt: "Above a ferromagnetic material's Curie temperature, it:", options: ["becomes a perfect diamagnet", "has a susceptibility that increases without bound", "loses its large spontaneous magnetization and behaves paramagnetically instead", "shows no change, since the Curie temperature has no physical significance"], answer: 2, hint: "Domain alignment competes against thermal agitation.", solution: "Above the Curie temperature thermal jostling overwhelms the domain-locking interaction, and the strong ferromagnetic response collapses into ordinary (much weaker) paramagnetic behavior.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["magnetic-materials", "ferromagnetism"] },
    { id: "em-faraday-d1", lesson: "em-faraday", type: "mc", prompt: "Lenz's law states that an induced current flows in the direction that:", options: ["Opposes the change in flux that caused it", "Reinforces the change in flux that caused it", "Is always clockwise", "Depends only on the wire's resistance"], answer: 0, hint: "Think about energy conservation.", solution: "The induced current's own field opposes the change in flux — a direct consequence of forbidding a self-amplifying (free-energy) feedback loop.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["faraday", "lenz"] },
    { id: "em-faraday-d2", lesson: "em-faraday", type: "numeric", prompt: "A loop of area $0.020\\,\\text{m}^2$ sits in a uniform field perpendicular to it that rises from $0.10\\,\\text{T}$ to $0.30\\,\\text{T}$ over $0.50\\,\\text{s}$. Find the magnitude of the induced EMF.", answer: 0.008, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "V", hint: "$\\varepsilon = \\Delta\\Phi/\\Delta t = A\\Delta B/\\Delta t$.", solution: "$\\varepsilon = (0.020)(0.30-0.10)/0.50 = (0.020)(0.20)/0.50 = 0.004/0.5 = 0.008\\,\\text{V}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["faraday", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-faraday-d3", lesson: "em-faraday", type: "mc", prompt: "A conducting rod slides freely (no external push) along frictionless rails in a uniform magnetic field, generating an induced current. The magnetic force on the rod:", options: ["Opposes the rod's motion, decelerating it", "Accelerates the rod in its direction of motion", "Is zero, since the rod is in free motion", "Acts perpendicular to the rails but not the rod's velocity"], answer: 0, hint: "Apply Lenz's law to the force, not just the current direction.", solution: "The induced current opposes the change in flux, and by $\\mathbf{F}=I\\mathbf{L}\\times\\mathbf{B}$ this manifests as a retarding force, converting kinetic energy to resistive heating.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["faraday", "lenz", "motional-emf"] },
    { id: "em-faraday-d4", lesson: "em-faraday", type: "mc", prompt: "A loop of area $A$ rotates at angular frequency $\\omega$ in a uniform field $B$, with $\\Phi=BA\\cos\\omega t$. The induced EMF is maximum when:", options: ["The loop's plane is parallel to $\\mathbf{B}$ (flux is instantaneously zero)", "The loop's plane is perpendicular to $\\mathbf{B}$ (flux is maximum)", "The EMF is always constant, independent of orientation", "Exactly halfway through a full rotation only"], answer: 0, hint: "$\\varepsilon = -d\\Phi/dt = BA\\omega\\sin\\omega t$; where is $\\sin$ maximized relative to $\\cos$?", solution: "$\\varepsilon\\propto\\sin\\omega t$, which peaks a quarter-cycle after $\\Phi=BA\\cos\\omega t$ peaks — i.e. exactly when flux is passing through zero, loop plane parallel to $B$.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["faraday", "generator"] },
    { id: "em-inductance-ac-d1", lesson: "em-inductance-ac", type: "mc", prompt: "In the mechanical-electrical analogy, self-inductance $L$ plays the role of:", options: ["Mass $m$", "Spring constant $k$", "Damping coefficient $b$", "Displacement $x$"], answer: 0, hint: "$\\varepsilon = -L\\,dI/dt$ mirrors $F=-m\\,dv/dt$-type inertia.", solution: "Inductance resists changes in current the way mass resists changes in velocity — both are inertia-like terms.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["inductance", "analogy"] },
    { id: "em-inductance-ac-d2", lesson: "em-inductance-ac", type: "numeric", prompt: "Find the energy stored in an inductor with $L=0.50\\,\\text{H}$ carrying a current of $2.0\\,\\text{A}$.", answer: 1, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "J", hint: "$U_L = \\tfrac12 LI^2$.", solution: "$U = \\tfrac12(0.50)(2.0)^2 = \\tfrac12(0.50)(4) = 1.0\\,\\text{J}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["inductance", "energy", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-inductance-ac-d3", lesson: "em-inductance-ac", type: "mc", prompt: "In a driven series RLC circuit, at resonance ($\\omega=1/\\sqrt{LC}$) the impedance magnitude is:", options: ["Minimized, equal to $R$", "Maximized", "Zero", "Equal to $\\omega L$"], answer: 0, hint: "Inductive and capacitive reactances cancel at resonance.", solution: "$\\omega L = 1/(\\omega C)$ at resonance, so the imaginary parts of $Z=R+i\\omega L - i/(\\omega C)$ cancel, leaving $Z=R$, the minimum possible magnitude.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["ac-circuits", "resonance"] },
    { id: "em-inductance-ac-d4", lesson: "em-inductance-ac", type: "mc", prompt: "As the driving frequency $\\omega\\to0$ in a series RLC circuit, the capacitor's impedance $Z_C=1/(i\\omega C)$:", options: ["Approaches infinity, behaving like an open circuit", "Approaches zero, behaving like a wire", "Stays finite and equal to $R$", "Becomes purely real and equal to $C$"], answer: 0, hint: "Consider the DC limit — how does a capacitor behave in steady state?", solution: "At low frequency (DC-like), the capacitor has time to fully charge and block current, so $Z_C\\to\\infty$ — consistent with the RC-circuit long-time limit.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["ac-circuits", "limiting-case"] },
    { id: "em-maxwell-d1", lesson: "em-maxwell", type: "mc", prompt: "The displacement current term was added to Ampère's law in order to:", options: ["Make the law give a consistent answer regardless of which surface bounded by the loop is used", "Account for resistive losses in wires", "Explain why magnetic monopoles don't exist", "Correct a units error in the original law"], answer: 0, hint: "Think about the charging-capacitor ambiguity.", solution: "Without it, a flat surface (through the wire) and a bulged surface (between capacitor plates) gave different enclosed currents for the same loop — a genuine inconsistency the new term fixes.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["maxwell", "displacement-current"] },
    { id: "em-maxwell-d2", lesson: "em-maxwell", type: "mc", prompt: "Gauss's law for magnetism, $\\oint\\mathbf{B}\\cdot d\\mathbf{A}=0$, expresses the fact that:", options: ["No isolated magnetic monopoles have ever been observed", "Magnetic fields do no work", "Current always flows in closed loops", "$\\mathbf{B}$ is always uniform"], answer: 0, hint: "Compare to Gauss's law for $\\mathbf{E}$, which is nonzero because charge can be isolated.", solution: "Unlike electric charge, magnetic 'charge' (a monopole) has never been isolated — field lines always close on themselves, giving zero net flux through any closed surface.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["maxwell", "monopoles"] },
    { id: "em-maxwell-d3", lesson: "em-maxwell", type: "mc", prompt: "According to the Ampère–Maxwell law, a magnetic field can be sourced by:", options: ["Real (conduction) current only", "A changing electric flux only", "Either real current or a changing electric flux", "Neither — only Faraday's law produces fields"], answer: 2, hint: "$\\oint\\mathbf{B}\\cdot d\\mathbf{l} = \\mu_0 I_{\\text{enc}} + \\mu_0\\epsilon_0\\,d\\Phi_E/dt$.", solution: "Both terms are sources of circulating $\\mathbf{B}$: real current (Ampère's original law) and the displacement current (changing $\\Phi_E$).", difficulty: 2, tags: ["maxwell", "ampere-maxwell"] },
    { id: "em-maxwell-d4", lesson: "em-maxwell", type: "mc", prompt: "For a capacitor being charged at rate $dQ/dt = I$, the displacement current through a surface between the plates equals:", options: ["$I$, exactly matching the conduction current in the wire", "Zero, since no charge moves between the plates", "$I/2$", "Infinite, since there is no conductor between the plates"], answer: 0, hint: "Compute $\\epsilon_0\\,d\\Phi_E/dt$ using $E=Q/(\\epsilon_0A)$.", solution: "$\\epsilon_0\\,d\\Phi_E/dt = \\epsilon_0 A\\,dE/dt = \\epsilon_0 A \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\epsilon_0A}\\dfrac{dQ}{dt} = \\dfrac{dQ}{dt}=I$ — the two surfaces agree exactly, as required for consistency.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["maxwell", "displacement-current", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-waves-d1", lesson: "em-waves", type: "mc", prompt: "The speed of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum, $c=1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon_0}$, was historically significant because:", options: ["$\\mu_0$ and $\\epsilon_0$ were measured in unrelated static-field experiments, yet their combination matched light's independently measured speed", "It was the first measurement of $\\mu_0$ ever made", "It proved light requires a medium", "It contradicted Faraday's law"], answer: 0, hint: "Where do $\\mu_0$ and $\\epsilon_0$ individually come from?", solution: "Both constants come from static-charge and steady-current experiments having nothing to do with optics, making the numerical match to $c$ strong evidence that light is an EM wave.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["em-waves", "maxwell"] },
    { id: "em-waves-d2", lesson: "em-waves", type: "mc", prompt: "In a plane electromagnetic wave in vacuum, the field magnitudes satisfy:", options: ["$E=cB$", "$E=B/c$", "$E=\\mu_0\\epsilon_0 B$", "$E$ and $B$ are independent"], answer: 0, hint: "Derived directly from combining Faraday's and Ampère-Maxwell's laws for the wave solution.", solution: "$E=cB$ holds at every point and instant for a plane EM wave in vacuum.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["em-waves"] },
    { id: "em-waves-d3", lesson: "em-waves", type: "numeric", prompt: "An EM wave has peak electric field $100\\,\\text{V/m}$. Estimate its intensity. (Use $\\mu_0 c \\approx 377\\,\\Omega$.)", answer: 13.3, tolerance: 0.05, unit: "W/m^2", hint: "$I = \\tfrac12 E_0^2/(\\mu_0 c)$.", solution: "$I = \\tfrac12(100)^2/377 = 5000/377 \\approx 13.3\\ \\text{W/m}^2$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["em-waves", "poynting", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-waves-d4", lesson: "em-waves", type: "mc", prompt: "A perfectly reflecting surface receives EM wave intensity $I$. The radiation pressure it experiences is:", options: ["$2I/c$", "$I/c$", "$I/2c$", "$Ic$"], answer: 0, hint: "Reflection reverses the wave's momentum, doubling the momentum transfer compared to absorption.", solution: "Absorption gives $P=I/c$ (momentum $p=U/c$ delivered and stopped); reflection reverses the wave's momentum, doubling the transfer to $2I/c$.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["em-waves", "radiation-pressure"] },
    { id: "em-multipoles-d1", lesson: "em-multipoles", type: "mc", prompt: "The leading (monopole) term of a far-field potential expansion, $V\\propto1/r$, is present only when:", options: ["The net enclosed charge is nonzero", "The distribution has a nonzero dipole moment", "The distribution is spherically symmetric", "The distribution is neutral"], answer: 0, hint: "Compare to a single point charge's potential.", solution: "The $1/r$ term's coefficient is the net charge $Q$; if $Q=0$ it vanishes and the next surviving term (dipole, $1/r^2$) takes over.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["multipoles"] },
    { id: "em-multipoles-d2", lesson: "em-multipoles", type: "mc", prompt: "A point charge $+q$ is held above an infinite grounded conducting plane. Its image charge is:", options: ["$-q$, at the mirror-image position behind the plane", "$+q$, at the mirror-image position", "$-q/2$, at the charge's own position", "There is no image charge for a grounded plane"], answer: 0, hint: "The image must make $V=0$ on the plane, matching the grounded boundary condition.", solution: "A charge $-q$ at the mirror position exactly cancels $V$ on the plane (equal and opposite contributions at every point on it), reproducing the grounded boundary condition.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["method-of-images"] },
    { id: "em-multipoles-d3", lesson: "em-multipoles", type: "numeric", prompt: "A charge of $1.0\\,\\mu\\text{C}$ is held $2.0\\,\\text{cm}$ above an infinite grounded conducting plane. Find the magnitude of the attractive force on it.", answer: 5.625, tolerance: 0.03, unit: "N", hint: "$F=kq^2/(2d)^2$, using the image charge at separation $2d$.", solution: "$F = \\dfrac{(9\\times10^9)(1\\times10^{-6})^2}{(0.04)^2} = \\dfrac{9\\times10^{-3}}{1.6\\times10^{-3}} = 5.625\\ \\text{N}$.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["method-of-images", "computation"] },
    { id: "em-multipoles-d4", lesson: "em-multipoles", type: "mc", prompt: "A charge distribution has zero net charge and zero dipole moment (by symmetry). Its far-field potential falls off as:", options: ["$1/r^3$ (quadrupole)", "$1/r$ (monopole)", "$1/r^2$ (dipole)", "It is exactly zero everywhere"], answer: 0, hint: "Both lower-order terms vanish; the next term in the hierarchy takes over.", solution: "With both monopole and dipole terms zero, the leading nonvanishing term is the quadrupole, $\\propto1/r^3$.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["multipoles"] }
  ],
  test: [
    { id: "em-t1", prompt: "Two point charges, each of magnitude $q$, are separated by $r$ and repel with force $F$. Both charges are tripled and the separation is halved. The new force is:", options: ["$F$", "$4F$", "$9F$", "$36F$", "$81F$"], answer: 3, solution: "$F'=k(3q)(3q)/(r/2)^2 = 9\\cdot4\\cdot kq^2/r^2 = 36F$.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["coulomb", "scaling"], lesson: "em-coulomb-field" },
    { id: "em-t2", prompt: "A solid sphere of radius $R$ carries total charge $Q$ spread uniformly through its volume. The field at $r=R/2$ (inside the sphere), compared to the field magnitude at the surface $E_s = kQ/R^2$, is:", options: ["$E_s/8$", "$E_s/4$", "$E_s/2$", "$E_s$", "$2E_s$"], answer: 2, solution: "Inside a uniform sphere, $E(r) = kQr/R^3$ (enclosed charge $\\propto r^3$ cancels one power of $1/r^2$, leaving one power of $r$). At $r=R/2$: $E = kQ(R/2)/R^3 = kQ/(2R^2) = E_s/2$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["gauss-law", "sphere"], lesson: "em-gauss" },
    { id: "em-t3", prompt: "The electric field outside an infinite uniformly charged line (linear density $\\lambda$), as a function of perpendicular distance $r$, falls off as:", options: ["$1/r$", "$1/r^2$", "$1/r^3$", "Constant, independent of $r$", "$r$"], answer: 0, solution: "A cylindrical Gaussian surface of radius $r$, length $L$, gives $E\\cdot2\\pi rL = \\lambda L/\\epsilon_0 \\Rightarrow E=\\lambda/(2\\pi\\epsilon_0 r)$, i.e. $1/r$ — the extra dimension of symmetry (line vs. point) softens the falloff from $1/r^2$ to $1/r$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["gauss-law", "cylinder"], lesson: "em-gauss" },
    { id: "em-t4", prompt: "A charge is moved along a closed loop in a static electric field, returning to its starting point. The net work done by the field is:", options: ["Zero, regardless of the path", "Positive, if the loop encloses positive charge", "Negative, if the charge is negative", "Dependent on the loop's area", "Dependent on the field strength along the path"], answer: 0, solution: "A static $\\mathbf{E}$ field is conservative, so work around any closed loop is exactly zero — a direct consequence of potential being single-valued.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["potential", "conservative-field"], lesson: "em-potential" },
    { id: "em-t5", prompt: "A proton is accelerated from rest through a potential difference of $200\\,\\text{V}$. Its final kinetic energy is closest to:", options: ["$3.2\\times10^{-17}\\,\\text{J}$", "$1.6\\times10^{-17}\\,\\text{J}$", "$6.4\\times10^{-17}\\,\\text{J}$", "$3.2\\times10^{-19}\\,\\text{J}$", "$1.6\\times10^{-19}\\,\\text{J}$"], answer: 0, solution: "$KE = qV = (1.6\\times10^{-19})(200) = 3.2\\times10^{-17}\\,\\text{J}$ (equivalently, $200\\,\\text{eV}$).", difficulty: 2, tags: ["potential", "energy", "computation"], lesson: "em-potential" },
    { id: "em-t6", prompt: "A $3\\,\\mu\\text{F}$ capacitor and a $6\\,\\mu\\text{F}$ capacitor are connected in series; that combination is placed in parallel with a $2\\,\\mu\\text{F}$ capacitor. The total capacitance is:", options: ["$1\\,\\mu\\text{F}$", "$2\\,\\mu\\text{F}$", "$4\\,\\mu\\text{F}$", "$6\\,\\mu\\text{F}$", "$11\\,\\mu\\text{F}$"], answer: 2, solution: "Series: $(3\\cdot6)/(3+6)=18/9=2\\,\\mu\\text{F}$. Parallel with $2\\,\\mu\\text{F}$: $2+2=4\\,\\mu\\text{F}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["capacitance", "networks"], lesson: "em-conductors-capacitors" },
    { id: "em-t7", prompt: "The field between two capacitor plates is $10^6\\,\\text{V/m}$ (near the breakdown field of air). The energy density stored in that field is closest to:", options: ["$4.4\\,\\text{J/m}^3$", "$0.44\\,\\text{J/m}^3$", "$44\\,\\text{J/m}^3$", "$8.9\\times10^{-6}\\,\\text{J/m}^3$", "$1.0\\times10^{6}\\,\\text{J/m}^3$"], answer: 0, solution: "$u=\\tfrac12\\epsilon_0E^2 = \\tfrac12(8.85\\times10^{-12})(10^6)^2 = \\tfrac12(8.85\\times10^{-12})(10^{12}) \\approx 4.4\\,\\text{J/m}^3$.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["energy-density", "estimation"], lesson: "em-conductors-capacitors" },
    { id: "em-t8", prompt: "A parallel-plate capacitor of total plate area $A$ and separation $d$ has a dielectric ($\\kappa=3$) filling exactly half the area, with air filling the other half (both halves span the full separation $d$). Compared to the same capacitor with no dielectric ($C_0=\\epsilon_0A/d$), the new capacitance is:", options: ["$C_0$", "$1.5\\,C_0$", "$2\\,C_0$", "$3\\,C_0$", "$4\\,C_0$"], answer: 2, solution: "The two halves act as capacitors in parallel (same $d$, each area $A/2$): $C = \\dfrac{\\epsilon_0(A/2)}{d} + \\dfrac{\\kappa\\epsilon_0(A/2)}{d} = \\dfrac{\\epsilon_0A}{2d}(1+\\kappa) = \\dfrac{C_0}{2}(1+3) = 2C_0$.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["dielectrics", "capacitance", "networks"], lesson: "em-dielectrics" },
    { id: "em-t9", prompt: "A $12\\,\\text{V}$ battery is in series with a $4\\,\\Omega$ resistor; that combination then splits into two parallel branches of $6\\,\\Omega$ and $3\\,\\Omega$, which reconnect to the battery. The current through the $3\\,\\Omega$ branch is closest to:", options: ["$1.33\\,\\text{A}$", "$2\\,\\text{A}$", "$0.67\\,\\text{A}$", "$4\\,\\text{A}$", "$0.5\\,\\text{A}$"], answer: 0, solution: "Parallel combo: $(6\\cdot3)/(6+3)=2\\,\\Omega$. Total resistance $4+2=6\\,\\Omega$, total current $12/6=2\\,\\text{A}$. Voltage across the parallel section: $2\\times2=4\\,\\text{V}$. Current through $3\\,\\Omega$: $4/3\\approx1.33\\,\\text{A}$.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["kirchhoff", "networks"], lesson: "em-circuits" },
    { id: "em-t10", prompt: "A charged capacitor discharges through a resistor with time constant $\\tau$. The fraction of the original charge remaining after $t=2\\tau$ is closest to:", options: ["14%", "25%", "37%", "50%", "68%"], answer: 0, solution: "$Q/Q_0 = e^{-t/\\tau} = e^{-2} \\approx 0.135$, about 14%.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["rc-circuit", "exponential-decay"], lesson: "em-circuits" },
    { id: "em-t11", prompt: "A $4\\,\\Omega$ resistor and a $6\\,\\Omega$ resistor are connected in series to a $10\\,\\text{V}$ battery. The current is:", options: ["$0.5\\,\\text{A}$", "$1\\,\\text{A}$", "$2\\,\\text{A}$", "$4\\,\\text{A}$", "$10\\,\\text{A}$"], answer: 1, solution: "Series resistance $=4+6=10\\,\\Omega$; $I=V/R=10/10=1\\,\\text{A}$.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["kirchhoff", "series-circuit"], lesson: "em-circuits" },
    { id: "em-t12", prompt: "A charged particle moves in a circle of radius $r=mv/(qB)$ in a uniform magnetic field. If $B$ is doubled (with $m$, $v$, $q$ unchanged), the radius becomes:", options: ["Twice as large", "Half as large", "Four times as large", "Unchanged", "One quarter as large"], answer: 1, solution: "$r\\propto1/B$, so doubling $B$ halves $r$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["cyclotron", "scaling"], lesson: "em-magnetic-force" },
    { id: "em-t13", prompt: "A straight wire of length $0.20\\,\\text{m}$ carries a current of $4.0\\,\\text{A}$ perpendicular to a uniform field of $0.50\\,\\text{T}$. The force on the wire is:", options: ["$0.40\\,\\text{N}$", "$0.10\\,\\text{N}$", "$2.0\\,\\text{N}$", "$0.040\\,\\text{N}$", "$4.0\\,\\text{N}$"], answer: 0, solution: "$F=BIL = (0.50)(4.0)(0.20) = 0.40\\,\\text{N}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["magnetic-force", "computation"], lesson: "em-magnetic-force" },
    { id: "em-t14", prompt: "A circular current loop of radius $R$ produces field $B=\\mu_0I/2R$ at its center. If the radius is doubled with the same current, the center field becomes:", options: ["Twice as large", "Half as large", "Four times as large", "One quarter as large", "Unchanged"], answer: 1, solution: "$B\\propto1/R$, so doubling $R$ halves $B$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["biot-savart", "scaling"], lesson: "em-magnetic-force" },
    { id: "em-t15", prompt: "A coil of $N$ turns and area $A$ rotates at angular frequency $\\omega$ in field $B$; its maximum EMF is $NBA\\omega$. If $\\omega$ is tripled (all else fixed), the maximum EMF:", options: ["Triples", "Becomes nine times as large", "Becomes one third as large", "Is unchanged", "Becomes one ninth as large"], answer: 0, solution: "Max EMF $\\propto\\omega$ (linear), so tripling $\\omega$ triples the maximum EMF.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["faraday", "generator", "scaling"], lesson: "em-faraday" },
    { id: "em-t16", prompt: "The south pole of a magnet approaches a wire loop, increasing the flux through it (taken as pointing toward the magnet). By Lenz's law, the face of the loop nearest the magnet becomes, in effect:", options: ["A south pole, repelling the approaching magnet", "A north pole, attracting the approaching magnet", "Unmagnetized, with no induced effect", "A pole matching the magnet's motion exactly, offering no resistance", "Alternating between poles rapidly"], answer: 0, solution: "The induced current opposes the increasing flux, which means the near face must repel the approaching south pole — i.e. it acts like a south pole facing a south pole.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["faraday", "lenz"], lesson: "em-faraday" },
    { id: "em-t17", prompt: "An LC circuit has $L=2.0\\,\\text{mH}$ and $C=8.0\\,\\mu\\text{F}$. Its resonant angular frequency is closest to:", options: ["$7.9\\times10^3\\,\\text{rad/s}$", "$7.9\\times10^2\\,\\text{rad/s}$", "$7.9\\times10^4\\,\\text{rad/s}$", "$1.6\\times10^{-8}\\,\\text{rad/s}$", "$2.5\\times10^2\\,\\text{rad/s}$"], answer: 0, solution: "$\\omega_0=1/\\sqrt{LC}=1/\\sqrt{(2\\times10^{-3})(8\\times10^{-6})}=1/\\sqrt{1.6\\times10^{-8}}\\approx1/(1.26\\times10^{-4})\\approx7.9\\times10^3\\,\\text{rad/s}$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["lc-circuit", "computation"], lesson: "em-inductance-ac" },
    { id: "em-t18", prompt: "A series RLC circuit is driven at a frequency below its resonant frequency $\\omega_0=1/\\sqrt{LC}$. The current relative to the driving voltage:", options: ["Lags the voltage, since the circuit is inductive below resonance", "Leads the voltage, since the circuit is capacitive below resonance", "Is exactly in phase, as at any frequency", "Leads the voltage by exactly $90°$", "Lags the voltage by exactly $90°$"], answer: 1, solution: "Below resonance, $1/(\\omega C) > \\omega L$, so the capacitive reactance dominates, making $Z$'s phase negative — the circuit is 'capacitive' and current leads voltage.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["ac-circuits", "phase"], lesson: "em-inductance-ac" },
    { id: "em-t19", prompt: "Of Maxwell's four equations, the one that required an added term (the displacement current) to be mutually consistent is:", options: ["Gauss's law for $\\mathbf{E}$", "Gauss's law for $\\mathbf{B}$", "Faraday's law", "Ampère's law", "None required modification"], answer: 3, solution: "Ampère's original law gave surface-dependent (inconsistent) answers for a charging capacitor; Maxwell fixed it by adding the displacement current term.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["maxwell"], lesson: "em-maxwell" },
    { id: "em-t20", prompt: "Using $\\mu_0\\approx1.3\\times10^{-6}\\,\\text{H/m}$ and $\\epsilon_0\\approx8.9\\times10^{-12}\\,\\text{F/m}$, the order of magnitude of $c=1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\epsilon_0}$ is:", options: ["$10^{6}\\,\\text{m/s}$", "$10^{7}\\,\\text{m/s}$", "$10^{8}\\,\\text{m/s}$", "$10^{9}\\,\\text{m/s}$", "$10^{10}\\,\\text{m/s}$"], answer: 2, solution: "$\\mu_0\\epsilon_0\\approx(1.3\\times10^{-6})(8.9\\times10^{-12})\\approx1.16\\times10^{-17}$; $\\sqrt{\\cdot}\\approx3.4\\times10^{-9}$; $1/(3.4\\times10^{-9})\\approx3\\times10^8\\,\\text{m/s}$, order $10^8$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["em-waves", "estimation"], lesson: "em-waves" },
    { id: "em-t21", prompt: "An isotropic point source radiates EM power $P$ uniformly in all directions. If the distance from the source triples, the intensity becomes:", options: ["One third", "One ninth", "One twenty-seventh", "Three times", "Nine times"], answer: 1, solution: "$I=P/(4\\pi r^2)\\propto1/r^2$; tripling $r$ divides intensity by $3^2=9$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["em-waves", "inverse-square"], lesson: "em-waves" },
    { id: "em-t22", prompt: "In a plane electromagnetic wave traveling through vacuum, the electric field, magnetic field, and propagation direction are, relative to one another:", options: ["Mutually perpendicular", "All parallel to one another", "$\\mathbf{E}$ and $\\mathbf{B}$ parallel, both perpendicular to propagation", "$\\mathbf{E}$ parallel to propagation, $\\mathbf{B}$ perpendicular to both", "Randomly oriented, varying wave to wave"], answer: 0, solution: "EM waves are transverse: $\\mathbf{E}$, $\\mathbf{B}$, and the propagation direction form a mutually perpendicular set.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["em-waves", "transverse"], lesson: "em-waves" },
    { id: "em-t23", prompt: "A point charge $q$ held at distance $d$ above a grounded conducting plane feels an attractive force $F$ from its image charge. If the charge is instead held at distance $2d$, the force becomes:", options: ["$F/2$", "$F/4$", "$F/8$", "$2F$", "$4F$"], answer: 1, solution: "$F=kq^2/(2d)^2 \\propto 1/d^2$; doubling $d$ divides the force by $2^2=4$.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["method-of-images", "scaling"], lesson: "em-multipoles" },
    { id: "em-t24", prompt: "The ratio of the electric force to the gravitational force between two protons (at any fixed separation) is closest to:", options: ["$10^{18}$", "$10^{27}$", "$10^{36}$", "$10^{45}$", "$10^{9}$"], answer: 2, solution: "$\\dfrac{F_E}{F_G} = \\dfrac{ke^2}{Gm_p^2} = \\dfrac{(9\\times10^9)(1.6\\times10^{-19})^2}{(6.7\\times10^{-11})(1.67\\times10^{-27})^2} \\approx \\dfrac{2.3\\times10^{-28}}{1.9\\times10^{-64}} \\approx 1.2\\times10^{36}$ — the separation cancels since both forces are $1/r^2$.", difficulty: 3, tags: ["estimation", "coulomb", "comparison"], lesson: "em-coulomb-field" },
    { id: "em-t25", prompt: "The product $RC$ (resistance times capacitance) has units of:", options: ["Seconds", "Ohms", "Farads", "Volts", "Coulombs"], answer: 0, solution: "$[R][C] = (\\text{V/A})(\\text{C/V}) = \\text{C/A} = \\text{s}$ (since $1\\,\\text{A}=1\\,\\text{C/s}$) — dimensionally, $RC$ must be a time, consistent with it being the exponential time constant.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["rc-circuit", "dimensional-analysis"], lesson: "em-circuits" },
    { id: "em-t26", prompt: "A solenoid with a vacuum core produces field $B_0$ for a given free current. An iron core with relative permeability $\\mu_r=600$ is inserted, current unchanged. The new field is:", options: ["$B_0/600$", "$B_0$, unchanged", "$600B_0$", "$\\sqrt{600}\\,B_0$", "$300B_0$"], answer: 2, solution: "$B=\\mu_rB_0$ for a linear magnetic material filling the solenoid, since the aligned atomic dipoles' own field adds to the vacuum result; $300B_0$ would be the result of a factor-of-two slip.", difficulty: 1, tags: ["magnetic-materials"], lesson: "em-fields-in-matter" },
    { id: "em-t27", prompt: "A paramagnetic material's susceptibility $\\chi$ is observed to fall as temperature rises (Curie's law, $\\chi\\propto1/T$). The best explanation is that:", options: ["The atomic dipole moments themselves shrink at higher $T$", "$\\chi$ becomes negative once $T$ is large enough", "The applied field itself weakens as $T$ rises", "Thermal agitation randomizes the dipoles' partial alignment with the field", "Higher $T$ converts the material to a diamagnet"], answer: 3, solution: "The dipoles themselves are unchanged; what falls with rising $T$ is how well the field can align them against increasing thermal jostling, which is exactly what Curie's law tracks.", difficulty: 2, tags: ["magnetic-materials"], lesson: "em-fields-in-matter" }
  ]
});
